1
00:00:05,591 --> 00:00:08,974
♪ In the not-too-distant future

2
00:00:09,009 --> 00:00:12,081
♪ Next Sunday A.D.

3
00:00:12,115 --> 00:00:15,187
♪ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:15,222 --> 00:00:17,914
♪ Not too different
from you or me

5
00:00:17,948 --> 00:00:20,951
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute

6
00:00:20,986 --> 00:00:24,300
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit

7
00:00:24,334 --> 00:00:26,992
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place

8
00:00:27,027 --> 00:00:32,032
♪ But his bosses didn't like
him so they shot him into space

9
00:00:33,826 --> 00:00:36,760
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies

10
00:00:36,795 --> 00:00:39,832
♪ The worst we can
find [la-la-la]

11
00:00:39,867 --> 00:00:42,732
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all

12
00:00:42,766 --> 00:00:46,253
♪ And we'll monitor
his mind [la-la-la]

13
00:00:46,287 --> 00:00:48,945
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control

14
00:00:48,979 --> 00:00:51,982
♪ Where the movies
begin or end [la-la-la]

15
00:00:52,017 --> 00:00:56,056
♪ Because he used
those special parts

16
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:00,198
♪ To make his robot friends

17
00:01:00,232 --> 00:01:02,200
♪ Robot roll call

18
00:01:02,234 --> 00:01:03,339
♪ Cambot
Pan left.

19
00:01:03,373 --> 00:01:04,581
♪ Gypsy
Hi, girl.

20
00:01:04,616 --> 00:01:06,480
♪ Tom Servo
What a cool guy.

21
00:01:06,514 --> 00:01:07,722
♪ Crow
A wisecracker.

22
00:01:07,757 --> 00:01:11,209
♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes

23
00:01:11,243 --> 00:01:14,315
♪ And other science
facts [la-la-la]

24
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:17,284
♪ Then repeat to
yourself it's just a show

25
00:01:17,318 --> 00:01:20,287
♪ I should really just relax

26
00:01:20,321 --> 00:01:25,119
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000

27
00:01:27,225 --> 00:01:31,712
[doors closing hydraulically]

28
00:01:35,129 --> 00:01:37,338
Hey, hi everybody. Welcome
to the Satellite of Love.

29
00:01:37,373 --> 00:01:39,133
I'm Joel Robinson and
these are my Bloods,

30
00:01:39,168 --> 00:01:40,548
Tom Servo and Crow.

31
00:01:40,583 --> 00:01:41,791
- Yo.
- Word.

32
00:01:41,825 --> 00:01:43,068
And we decided to
take the prefixes,

33
00:01:43,103 --> 00:01:45,898
root words and suffixes
of Mex-American food,

34
00:01:45,933 --> 00:01:48,108
add some new food
types, mix 'em all up

35
00:01:48,142 --> 00:01:50,351
and do what Madison Avenue
does and find a whole

36
00:01:50,386 --> 00:01:52,388
new way to love
the same old crap.

37
00:01:52,422 --> 00:01:53,768
Yeah, like the pundits say,

38
00:01:53,803 --> 00:01:55,045
invent a new kind
of Mexican junk food

39
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:57,289
and the world will make
a run for your border.

40
00:01:57,324 --> 00:01:59,360
Right, check
this one out, okay?

41
00:01:59,395 --> 00:02:02,881
Oh look, spicy,
peachy, battered changa.

42
00:02:02,915 --> 00:02:05,780
Oh, that would a spicy,
lightly-battered peach

43
00:02:05,815 --> 00:02:07,575
in a delicious, chewy-changa.

44
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:09,094
What the hell is a changa?

45
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:11,096
Hey, come on.
Leave him alone, he's
just getting into it.

46
00:02:11,131 --> 00:02:12,235
Try this one, Crow.

47
00:02:12,270 --> 00:02:14,962
Oh, chicken-y Gatorade-arita.

48
00:02:14,996 --> 00:02:17,033
Crispy dead chicken,
Gatorade marinade,

49
00:02:17,067 --> 00:02:19,277
all wrapped in a tasty,
toasty tacorito pita.

50
00:02:19,311 --> 00:02:22,107
Oh! Okay, what
do we have here?

51
00:02:22,142 --> 00:02:24,282
Oh look, bel grande, cheesy--

52
00:02:24,316 --> 00:02:25,628
Did we use cheesy yet?

53
00:02:25,662 --> 00:02:27,216
Yeah, well not
yet, here it is.

54
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:30,598
Okay, beefy blue steak taco.

55
00:02:30,633 --> 00:02:31,530
Oh good, we're on a roll.

56
00:02:31,565 --> 00:02:35,638
Bel rocco, poco, loco, roccoco--

57
00:02:35,672 --> 00:02:37,053
Hey, I'm running out of room.

58
00:02:37,087 --> 00:02:39,089
That's okay, keep flowin'
with it. Taco! [laughs]

59
00:02:39,124 --> 00:02:40,815
Ooh, it's cocomoco good!

60
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:41,851
[all] Olé!

61
00:02:41,885 --> 00:02:44,164
Olé off. Commercial
sign in 15 seconds.

62
00:02:44,198 --> 00:02:47,339
Oh, well here's my favorite
right here. Check this out.

63
00:02:47,374 --> 00:02:50,756
Beefy, peanut
buster, bel grande!

64
00:02:50,791 --> 00:02:53,552
Oh, you get a mister
misdemeanor for that beater.

65
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:55,451
- Ore-Ida.
- Orita!

66
00:02:55,485 --> 00:02:57,038
Goodnight-a.

67
00:02:57,073 --> 00:03:01,181
[new wave music]

68
00:03:02,285 --> 00:03:05,737
Well, this one's
the Pat Morita fajita.

69
00:03:05,771 --> 00:03:07,532
[robots laugh]
That's cute.

70
00:03:07,566 --> 00:03:12,502
And then there's the
Maria Conchita Alonzarito.

71
00:03:12,537 --> 00:03:13,814
Cute, cute,
that's a good one.

72
00:03:13,848 --> 00:03:14,815
Here's one of my favorites,

73
00:03:14,849 --> 00:03:20,096
the Emperor Hirohito-rito.

74
00:03:20,130 --> 00:03:24,204
And the Curly Joe Dorito.

75
00:03:24,238 --> 00:03:26,275
And not to forget the

76
00:03:26,309 --> 00:03:31,176
Ed Herlihy-ito Dorito.

77
00:03:31,211 --> 00:03:34,524
Made with fine
Kraft Jet-Puffed
miniature marshmallows.

78
00:03:34,559 --> 00:03:37,147
[laughs] Say Joel,
why do Americans

79
00:03:37,182 --> 00:03:38,942
rename Mexican food dishes?

80
00:03:38,977 --> 00:03:40,668
Oh, it's just part
of the American way.

81
00:03:40,703 --> 00:03:42,981
Turning a neighboring
country rich in

82
00:03:43,015 --> 00:03:45,639
culture and beauty
into a goofy appetizer.

83
00:03:45,673 --> 00:03:47,744
Oops, I think the
Mads are calling.

84
00:03:47,779 --> 00:03:50,920
Happy hour is
over, taco beelzebub.

85
00:03:50,954 --> 00:03:52,335
You know, in your
little food talk,

86
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:53,785
you failed to mention
that breakfast is still

87
00:03:53,819 --> 00:03:55,614
the most important
meal of the day.

88
00:03:55,649 --> 00:03:56,788
Isn't that right, Frank?

89
00:03:56,822 --> 00:03:58,203
Yes sir. As a matter
of fact, I usually try

90
00:03:58,238 --> 00:04:02,414
to have my breakfast first
thing in the morning [laughs].

91
00:04:02,449 --> 00:04:03,933
Which, for me is usually about

92
00:04:03,967 --> 00:04:08,317
2:30 in the afternoon [laughs].

93
00:04:08,351 --> 00:04:11,181
[laughs turn into cries]

94
00:04:11,216 --> 00:04:13,218
Oh God, it hurts.

95
00:04:13,253 --> 00:04:17,118
There, there. Why don't
you run along and play, Frank.

96
00:04:17,153 --> 00:04:19,293
Thanks, Steve.

97
00:04:19,328 --> 00:04:20,363
He's in therapy.

98
00:04:20,398 --> 00:04:24,125
Well anyway, our invention
exchange this week is

99
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:27,094
for family members who
won't come in for breakfast.

100
00:04:27,128 --> 00:04:32,341
You bring breakfast to them
with the breakfast bazooka.

101
00:04:32,375 --> 00:04:35,240
Oh Frank, come in for breakfast!

102
00:04:35,275 --> 00:04:36,379
I'm playing!

103
00:04:36,414 --> 00:04:41,142
You die Joe!
[bazooka fires]

104
00:04:42,351 --> 00:04:44,180
This delicious meal
has a flat trajectory

105
00:04:44,214 --> 00:04:47,839
and a muzzle velocity of
2,400 feet per second.

106
00:04:47,873 --> 00:04:50,704
It's all part of
this nutritious war.

107
00:04:50,738 --> 00:04:51,808
What do you think?

108
00:04:51,843 --> 00:04:53,120
What a coinkydink.

109
00:04:53,154 --> 00:04:55,087
Our invention is based
on a similar concept.

110
00:04:55,122 --> 00:04:56,813
That's right, it's for
the hurried youth of today,

111
00:04:56,848 --> 00:04:59,920
so busy, they sometimes
forget to stuff their faces

112
00:04:59,954 --> 00:05:03,199
with toxic, chemically-laced,
life-shortening sugar snacks.

113
00:05:03,233 --> 00:05:06,409
Yeah, it's called
a between-meal
mortar, check it out.

114
00:05:06,444 --> 00:05:09,170
Clear! [mortar fires]

115
00:05:09,205 --> 00:05:10,206
[all scream]

116
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:11,034
It's a live one!

117
00:05:11,069 --> 00:05:13,485
Look out guys, I'll save you!

118
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:14,900
[loud explosion]

119
00:05:14,935 --> 00:05:18,110
[all coughing and groaning]

120
00:05:18,145 --> 00:05:20,458
They say you never hear the
snack with your name on it.

121
00:05:20,492 --> 00:05:21,942
What do you think, sirs?

122
00:05:21,976 --> 00:05:25,014
Well, Julia Childish,
your experiment this week

123
00:05:25,048 --> 00:05:28,880
is an unwanted, uncalled-for,
unnecessary sequel

124
00:05:28,914 --> 00:05:32,918
to The Amazing Colossal Man,
War of the Colossal Beast.

125
00:05:32,953 --> 00:05:35,300
But first, a little
musical nightmare

126
00:05:35,335 --> 00:05:37,198
from the heart of whiteness.

127
00:05:37,233 --> 00:05:38,614
Bon appe-die.

128
00:05:38,648 --> 00:05:40,063
Oh cripes,
these buffets on my

129
00:05:40,098 --> 00:05:42,100
body are so
reasonable, you know?

130
00:05:42,134 --> 00:05:43,101
You know, Marge
and I came here

131
00:05:43,135 --> 00:05:44,896
one time we brought the men in.

132
00:05:44,930 --> 00:05:46,138
Oh cripes, you gotta try some

133
00:05:46,173 --> 00:05:48,486
of their Rice Krispies
down by the belly button.

134
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:49,659
[buzzing and screaming]

135
00:05:49,694 --> 00:05:52,075
Oh, we've got
movie sign! [screams]

136
00:05:52,110 --> 00:05:56,873
[doors opening hydraulically]

137
00:06:00,843 --> 00:06:01,982
[all coughing and spitting]

138
00:06:02,016 --> 00:06:03,224
Just get it out of
my valves, will you?

139
00:06:03,259 --> 00:06:06,366
Oh, Mr. Natural. I remember
him from Zap Comix.

140
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,816
[marching band music]

141
00:06:08,851 --> 00:06:12,061
CONN LTD. and the
New Power Generation.

142
00:06:12,095 --> 00:06:14,684
CONN!

143
00:06:14,719 --> 00:06:17,963
And the Republican
High School marching band!

144
00:06:17,998 --> 00:06:20,241
Betty Luster, was she
married to Martin Sheen?

145
00:06:20,276 --> 00:06:22,554
Think so [laughs].

146
00:06:22,589 --> 00:06:24,073
Oh, look at that guy.

147
00:06:24,107 --> 00:06:26,524
Otto Krauschaar!

148
00:06:26,558 --> 00:06:28,905
What does A.S.C. mean?

149
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:32,081
- A sick cookie.
- Oh.

150
00:06:32,115 --> 00:06:36,534
[all sing] Come on and
buy some crap from us.

151
00:06:36,568 --> 00:06:40,020
You know that you want to.

152
00:06:40,054 --> 00:06:43,506
And the white race
will salute you

153
00:06:43,541 --> 00:06:47,993
as you prance and gad about.

154
00:06:48,028 --> 00:06:49,132
Thank you.
[flute music]

155
00:06:49,167 --> 00:06:50,720
Huh?

156
00:06:50,755 --> 00:06:52,929
Boy, am I glad to see you!

157
00:06:52,964 --> 00:06:54,206
Well, it's not mutual.

158
00:06:54,241 --> 00:06:56,277
What's that? What do
you mean, "Who's talking"?

159
00:06:56,312 --> 00:06:57,140
Yeah.

160
00:06:57,175 --> 00:06:59,626
It's me, that's
who. Mr. B Natural.

161
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,075
Second note from the left.

162
00:07:01,110 --> 00:07:02,870
As natural a B
as you'll ever see.

163
00:07:02,905 --> 00:07:03,906
Joel, I'm scared.

164
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:06,149
Wait, hold the music!

165
00:07:06,184 --> 00:07:07,496
Oh, thank you.

166
00:07:07,530 --> 00:07:08,669
Maybe we can make things
a little clearer around here.

167
00:07:08,704 --> 00:07:10,740
[flute plays]
[all scream]

168
00:07:10,775 --> 00:07:12,190
- How's that?
- It stinks.

169
00:07:12,224 --> 00:07:14,054
Now, move in a
little closer, friend.

170
00:07:14,088 --> 00:07:16,228
- No, no, don't.
- Let's get acquainted.

171
00:07:16,263 --> 00:07:17,126
No, let's not.

172
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:20,440
Mr. B Natural,
at your service.

173
00:07:20,474 --> 00:07:22,545
The spirit of
music, that's me.

174
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,098
The spirit of fun in music.

175
00:07:24,133 --> 00:07:25,134
Fun.

176
00:07:25,168 --> 00:07:27,101
And I've been around
an awful long time.

177
00:07:27,136 --> 00:07:27,930
Mm-hmm.

178
00:07:27,964 --> 00:07:29,069
Knew your father, I did.

179
00:07:29,103 --> 00:07:30,898
Hey, leave my
father out of this!

180
00:07:30,933 --> 00:07:32,141
And don't be
too sure I wasn't

181
00:07:32,175 --> 00:07:34,143
in the garden with
Mr. and Mrs. Adam.

182
00:07:34,177 --> 00:07:35,627
[shudders] You
were the snake.

183
00:07:35,662 --> 00:07:37,146
Why, you should know me.

184
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:39,355
The spirit of music's
inside all of you.

185
00:07:39,389 --> 00:07:40,183
- Mm-hmm.
- No, I bathe.

186
00:07:40,218 --> 00:07:41,599
In you.

187
00:07:41,633 --> 00:07:43,324
In you.

188
00:07:43,359 --> 00:07:44,947
In all of you.

189
00:07:44,981 --> 00:07:49,572
Right there, waiting to be
found, waiting to be expressed.

190
00:07:49,607 --> 00:07:50,780
Waiting to die.

191
00:07:50,815 --> 00:07:52,126
All you've gotta
do is to want music.

192
00:07:52,161 --> 00:07:53,058
[robot laughs]

193
00:07:53,093 --> 00:07:55,026
- To want the fun of it.
- That's great.

194
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:57,304
And when you
do, just call on me

195
00:07:57,338 --> 00:07:59,271
and I'll pop right
up to help you.

196
00:07:59,306 --> 00:08:00,652
No, that's okay.

197
00:08:00,687 --> 00:08:02,827
Generally, I live
in this music staff.

198
00:08:02,861 --> 00:08:04,932
But when I get a
message for help,

199
00:08:04,967 --> 00:08:08,039
I go off to appear
before people.

200
00:08:08,073 --> 00:08:11,456
Especially young people, when I
think I can do 'em some good.

201
00:08:11,491 --> 00:08:12,940
[robot laughs]
Oh, wonderful.

202
00:08:12,975 --> 00:08:15,080
Oh, I've known an awful
lot of people in my time.

203
00:08:15,115 --> 00:08:17,186
Got a whale of a lot of
swell stories to tell.

204
00:08:17,220 --> 00:08:18,532
I bet.

205
00:08:18,567 --> 00:08:21,155
One of the nicest is about
my good friend Buzz Turner,

206
00:08:21,190 --> 00:08:24,262
little guy I first met
when he was in junior high.

207
00:08:24,296 --> 00:08:27,161
Mr. B, you're hot.

208
00:08:27,196 --> 00:08:28,577
- Wanna see what happened?
- No.

209
00:08:28,611 --> 00:08:30,889
Come on, let's go meet Buzz.

210
00:08:30,924 --> 00:08:31,718
That's okay.
[flute plays]

211
00:08:31,752 --> 00:08:33,789
[all scream]

212
00:08:33,823 --> 00:08:36,101
Don't do that!

213
00:08:37,102 --> 00:08:38,518
[school bell rings]
I got a bad feeling.

214
00:08:38,552 --> 00:08:40,658
Did you ever notice the
excitement in a school corridor

215
00:08:40,692 --> 00:08:42,901
- at the end of the day?
- Oh yeah.

216
00:08:42,936 --> 00:08:45,386
More energy than you
can shake a stick at.

217
00:08:45,421 --> 00:08:48,079
The kind of energy that makes
fun and laughter and music.

218
00:08:48,113 --> 00:08:49,529
Hey, tone it down, lady.

219
00:08:49,563 --> 00:08:52,704
They're all there, with
all their young energy.

220
00:08:52,739 --> 00:08:55,120
The first time I
really met Buzz Turner,

221
00:08:55,155 --> 00:08:58,261
including Jeanie, the
cutest girl in school.

222
00:08:58,296 --> 00:09:00,574
- I'’ll say.
- Including Buzz.

223
00:09:00,609 --> 00:09:02,956
- Nothing much wrong with Buzz.
- Except his hair.

224
00:09:02,990 --> 00:09:04,198
A good 12-year-old kid

225
00:09:04,233 --> 00:09:06,511
with a lot of normal
12-year-old problems.

226
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:07,408
Buzz was a slacker.

227
00:09:07,443 --> 00:09:09,721
Just a little shy, maybe,

228
00:09:09,756 --> 00:09:11,620
wondering what it
takes to become part

229
00:09:11,654 --> 00:09:14,070
of the group, to really belong.

230
00:09:14,105 --> 00:09:18,454
- Conform. Conform.
- Conform. Conform.

231
00:09:18,488 --> 00:09:21,043
A kid who's searching
for the right answers.

232
00:09:21,077 --> 00:09:23,390
- It hurts a little.
- Hurts, don't it?

233
00:09:23,424 --> 00:09:25,081
How do you like
the new clarinet, Jim?

234
00:09:25,116 --> 00:09:26,842
- Hey.
- Oh, let's see, Fred.

235
00:09:26,876 --> 00:09:29,085
Got it from
the Franklin Mint.

236
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:30,639
You should hear
the tone, wow.

237
00:09:30,673 --> 00:09:34,056
Hey Peg, wanna see the
step I was telling you about?

238
00:09:34,090 --> 00:09:36,092
- Oh sure.
- It's a goose-step.

239
00:09:36,127 --> 00:09:39,199
- Make some music, huh?
- All right, let's go.

240
00:09:39,233 --> 00:09:40,027
Eins, zwei.

241
00:09:40,062 --> 00:09:42,202
That's not how you play it.

242
00:09:42,236 --> 00:09:44,894
Eins, zwei. Oh,
to be like them.

243
00:09:44,929 --> 00:09:47,172
He's got a gun! Oh, no.

244
00:09:47,207 --> 00:09:50,072
[kids cheering]
Thought he had a gun there.

245
00:09:50,106 --> 00:09:51,349
Hey gang, tell you what.

246
00:09:51,383 --> 00:09:53,006
How about coming to
my house for a while?

247
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:54,904
I got some great new records.

248
00:09:54,939 --> 00:09:56,147
Not me, Jeanie.

249
00:09:56,181 --> 00:09:58,494
Band practice, I wouldn't
miss it for anything.

250
00:09:58,528 --> 00:10:00,738
I'm buckin' for the
first clarinet chair.

251
00:10:00,772 --> 00:10:02,015
Oh, do you dare?

252
00:10:02,049 --> 00:10:03,741
Come on, Betty. Grab
your horn or we'll be late.

253
00:10:03,775 --> 00:10:07,054
- Okay, bye.
- Bye, so long.

254
00:10:07,089 --> 00:10:08,124
- They're a fun group.
- Yeah.

255
00:10:08,159 --> 00:10:09,816
These records
are the greatest.

256
00:10:09,850 --> 00:10:15,062
- Let's see now, we've got two.
- Wanna come? We can dance.

257
00:10:15,097 --> 00:10:16,823
- Don't hit me!
- No thanks.

258
00:10:16,857 --> 00:10:21,172
I don't know how, well, I
mean, I got some reading to do.

259
00:10:21,206 --> 00:10:22,760
You know, that
big history essay.

260
00:10:22,794 --> 00:10:25,141
But that's not
due for two weeks.

261
00:10:25,176 --> 00:10:28,179
I know Jeanie. Well,
I wanna get started.

262
00:10:28,213 --> 00:10:30,008
I gotta finish my
letter to Jodie Foster.

263
00:10:30,043 --> 00:10:35,013
- Well, come on then.
- What are we waiting for?

264
00:10:35,048 --> 00:10:37,050
That hurt. I'm
all messed up inside.

265
00:10:37,084 --> 00:10:41,537
If only an androgynous man
would come and visit me.

266
00:10:43,366 --> 00:10:48,924
I better wash my gym
clothes, it's been six months.

267
00:10:48,958 --> 00:10:52,859
Meanwhile, the Midvale
Police visit his locker.

268
00:10:52,893 --> 00:10:57,173
Find out why they call him Buzz.

269
00:10:57,208 --> 00:11:01,626
[trumpets sound]
[all] No!

270
00:11:01,661 --> 00:11:02,869
He's coming out of the closet.

271
00:11:02,903 --> 00:11:04,077
Looks like this boy
might be needing me.

272
00:11:04,111 --> 00:11:05,906
Well, that's what I'm here for.

273
00:11:05,941 --> 00:11:07,459
- I feel ill.
- Me too.

274
00:11:07,494 --> 00:11:09,807
Better wait 'til
he calls on me though,

275
00:11:09,841 --> 00:11:13,017
'til he reaches for the spirit.

276
00:11:13,051 --> 00:11:14,950
Calls for Satan [laughs].

277
00:11:14,984 --> 00:11:16,123
All right dear.

278
00:11:16,158 --> 00:11:18,332
Yeah, there's a weirdo
loose in the school.

279
00:11:18,367 --> 00:11:20,576
He was hiding in
Junior's locker.

280
00:11:20,610 --> 00:11:21,819
We've gotta call the police.

281
00:11:21,853 --> 00:11:24,062
Wait, I'm not, really, I
wouldn't want you to hang up.

282
00:11:24,097 --> 00:11:26,651
I'm not feeling--

283
00:11:26,686 --> 00:11:27,997
Nice house.

284
00:11:28,032 --> 00:11:30,517
Do they live in Pee-wee's
Playhouse? [laughs]

285
00:11:30,551 --> 00:11:34,866
- So early, dear?
- Yeah, I got some readin'.

286
00:11:34,901 --> 00:11:36,178
Well, it's such
a beautiful day,

287
00:11:36,212 --> 00:11:37,420
Daddy won't be home 'til six.

288
00:11:37,455 --> 00:11:39,457
I'm feeling very
sebaceous today, Mom.

289
00:11:39,491 --> 00:11:42,115
Better go up to my
room and do the reading.

290
00:11:42,149 --> 00:11:44,082
All right, son. Buzz?

291
00:11:44,117 --> 00:11:45,394
Don't make so much
noise when you read.

292
00:11:45,428 --> 00:11:47,051
Maybe we'll all go for
a nice ride after dinner

293
00:11:47,085 --> 00:11:49,536
- if Daddy's not too tired.
- Okay.

294
00:11:49,570 --> 00:11:55,059
Why does my kid have to
be such a dud? I was popular.

295
00:11:55,093 --> 00:11:57,958
[gasps]
Where'd you come from?

296
00:11:57,993 --> 00:11:59,132
Bellevue [laughs].

297
00:11:59,166 --> 00:12:01,375
Out there somewhere.
Do I look funny?

298
00:12:01,410 --> 00:12:02,376
You look hot.

299
00:12:02,411 --> 00:12:04,965
Well, it's kind
of a surprise.

300
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,967
- I'’ll say.
- Mr. B what?

301
00:12:07,002 --> 00:12:09,590
Natural. Shouldn't
be surprised, boy.

302
00:12:09,625 --> 00:12:12,076
Whether you know it or
not, you sent for me.

303
00:12:12,110 --> 00:12:13,629
When you reached for
that music over there

304
00:12:13,663 --> 00:12:16,908
to make you feel better,
I got your message.

305
00:12:16,943 --> 00:12:18,013
You awakened me.

306
00:12:18,047 --> 00:12:19,221
So I'm attracted to guys now?

307
00:12:19,255 --> 00:12:20,463
You mean you've
been hiding here

308
00:12:20,498 --> 00:12:21,671
in my room all that time
and I never even noticed?

309
00:12:21,706 --> 00:12:24,122
You've seen me do everything?

310
00:12:24,157 --> 00:12:26,090
No boy, I don't mean that.

311
00:12:26,124 --> 00:12:30,163
I mean, you awakened the
spirit of music inside you.

312
00:12:30,197 --> 00:12:31,923
That's me. Your spirit of music.

313
00:12:31,958 --> 00:12:33,131
He played the
devil's tritone.

314
00:12:33,166 --> 00:12:36,514
That's my name,
boy, B Natural.

315
00:12:36,548 --> 00:12:37,446
Oh, mister lady.

316
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:38,619
Why there's
nothing more natural

317
00:12:38,654 --> 00:12:41,346
than feelings of music
inside of people.

318
00:12:41,381 --> 00:12:45,109
Look here, boy. You sing a
baby a lullaby, and it coos.

319
00:12:45,143 --> 00:12:46,179
The lulla-baby-by.

320
00:12:46,213 --> 00:12:47,456
Expressing your
feelings with music

321
00:12:47,490 --> 00:12:50,217
is just as natural
as you can be.

322
00:12:50,252 --> 00:12:54,049
Gee, I don't know Mr. B,
I guess I like music okay.

323
00:12:54,083 --> 00:12:55,982
With the thing,
just please leave.

324
00:12:56,016 --> 00:12:58,260
But I don't know about
making music myself.

325
00:12:58,294 --> 00:13:00,918
I don't think I'd be any good.

326
00:13:00,952 --> 00:13:03,713
A boy talking
like that, blasphemy.

327
00:13:03,748 --> 00:13:05,405
Spanking time!

328
00:13:05,439 --> 00:13:07,614
I've been visiting with
an 8-year-old friend of mine

329
00:13:07,648 --> 00:13:09,685
- who woke me up recently.
- Oh God, no. No.

330
00:13:09,719 --> 00:13:12,101
Talked him into playing
the French horn, I did.

331
00:13:12,136 --> 00:13:15,173
You should hear him
[mimics French horn].

332
00:13:15,208 --> 00:13:17,141
He sucks, Mr. B Natural.

333
00:13:17,175 --> 00:13:20,040
Took to it like that,
because he wanted to.

334
00:13:20,075 --> 00:13:21,352
He wanted the fun of it.

335
00:13:21,386 --> 00:13:23,975
That's why he can play
that piece so easy.

336
00:13:24,010 --> 00:13:27,220
Wait'll he grows up and finds
out how hard it is. [laughs]

337
00:13:27,254 --> 00:13:30,016
That's very clever. Mom. Mom?

338
00:13:30,050 --> 00:13:33,743
Just because he's
young, he's not afraid.

339
00:13:33,778 --> 00:13:34,848
Wish I could.

340
00:13:34,883 --> 00:13:36,160
Wear tights
and prance around.

341
00:13:36,194 --> 00:13:40,405
Wish I could get in a band
or something and be as...

342
00:13:42,752 --> 00:13:44,237
well, be like Fred is.

343
00:13:44,271 --> 00:13:48,206
Now, none of that. None of
that. I'm here to help you,

344
00:13:48,241 --> 00:13:50,795
not to help you feel
sorry for yourself.

345
00:13:52,210 --> 00:13:54,212
- Ugh.
- Sure, you can be popular.

346
00:13:54,247 --> 00:13:55,731
- Sure, you can be in a band.
- Oh, Mr. B.

347
00:13:55,765 --> 00:13:58,113
And wait'll you see the kicks
you'll get out of it, Buzz.

348
00:13:58,147 --> 00:13:59,908
- Kicks.
- The glamour of the uniform,

349
00:13:59,942 --> 00:14:03,152
the thrill of traveling
for band competitions--

350
00:14:03,187 --> 00:14:05,223
The all-night coke
jags in cheap hotels.

351
00:14:05,258 --> 00:14:07,122
Just like being a
member of a football team,

352
00:14:07,156 --> 00:14:09,883
and most of all
Buzz, fun, fun, fun.

353
00:14:09,918 --> 00:14:13,093
That's nice. Mom!

354
00:14:13,128 --> 00:14:14,543
And I can do it for
you if you want me to,

355
00:14:14,577 --> 00:14:17,477
if you'll keep me
awake inside there.

356
00:14:17,511 --> 00:14:18,927
Bad touch.

357
00:14:20,238 --> 00:14:21,481
Are you a musician, Mr. B?

358
00:14:21,515 --> 00:14:22,723
Do you play any instruments?

359
00:14:22,758 --> 00:14:23,863
No, I'm in marketing.

360
00:14:23,897 --> 00:14:29,144
Me? They're members of my
family, boy. I love 'em all.

361
00:14:29,178 --> 00:14:32,837
Watch. Presto.

362
00:14:32,872 --> 00:14:35,115
- Allegro.
- Mom!

363
00:14:35,150 --> 00:14:37,704
Vivace.
[trumpets sound]

364
00:14:37,738 --> 00:14:38,601
Don't hurt me!

365
00:14:38,636 --> 00:14:40,189
Gosh, Mr. B, you
even talk music.

366
00:14:40,224 --> 00:14:41,363
You be bad.

367
00:14:41,397 --> 00:14:43,365
Anybody can,
Buzz. Be natural.

368
00:14:43,399 --> 00:14:45,919
Talk out what you
feel with music.

369
00:14:45,954 --> 00:14:49,095
Look here. Do you think
this is just a trumpet?

370
00:14:49,129 --> 00:14:50,337
- No.
- It's a bong!

371
00:14:50,372 --> 00:14:52,098
It's the feelings
that you want to feel,

372
00:14:52,132 --> 00:14:53,962
the things you want
to say to the world.

373
00:14:53,996 --> 00:14:57,034
Put a trumpet to your
lips and it becomes pride,

374
00:14:57,068 --> 00:15:00,244
confidence, the mood
of a happy king.

375
00:15:00,278 --> 00:15:04,800
[trumpet music]

376
00:15:04,834 --> 00:15:07,216
Oh my god, please say
this isn't happening.

377
00:15:07,251 --> 00:15:08,666
That's a happy king?

378
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:13,878
Mr. B, shame on you. Have
you no sense of decency?

379
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:20,160
A clarinet's not just a
clarinet, it's a happy smile!

380
00:15:20,195 --> 00:15:23,370
[clarinet music]

381
00:15:23,405 --> 00:15:27,961
[robots laugh]
He's so perky. Kill her.

382
00:15:30,032 --> 00:15:33,035
See, Buzz? It's really
fun to be psychotic [laughs].

383
00:15:33,070 --> 00:15:34,174
A trombone can let you

384
00:15:34,209 --> 00:15:37,764
show the world a
big laugh [laughs].

385
00:15:37,798 --> 00:15:40,491
[trombone music]

386
00:15:40,525 --> 00:15:42,389
Oh no.

387
00:15:43,804 --> 00:15:45,013
Why, Joel, why?

388
00:15:45,047 --> 00:15:48,948
When you want to
show dignity, Buzz,
try a French horn.

389
00:15:48,982 --> 00:15:50,087
[horn music]

390
00:15:50,121 --> 00:15:53,400
Mr. B, what would
you know about dignity?

391
00:15:53,435 --> 00:15:56,403
In tights and a hat.

392
00:15:56,438 --> 00:16:01,098
Buzz? When you want
to feel as big as giant,

393
00:16:01,132 --> 00:16:04,825
blow a sousaphone.
[sousaphone music]

394
00:16:04,860 --> 00:16:06,689
Now you're living in
your own fantasy world.

395
00:16:06,724 --> 00:16:09,761
We're into a whole
weird area here.

396
00:16:10,935 --> 00:16:11,971
Put 'em all together, Buzz,

397
00:16:12,005 --> 00:16:14,421
and you have any mood
you want to have fun.

398
00:16:14,456 --> 00:16:17,010
I'll talk, I'll
talk, don't hurt me!

399
00:16:17,045 --> 00:16:18,598
[marching band music]

400
00:16:18,632 --> 00:16:20,117
You know, I think Oscar Wilde

401
00:16:20,151 --> 00:16:22,705
only wished he was
this gay, you know?

402
00:16:22,740 --> 00:16:24,052
Ladies and
gentlemen, please accept

403
00:16:24,086 --> 00:16:27,710
our sincere apology
for all of this.

404
00:16:27,745 --> 00:16:28,988
Please.

405
00:16:29,954 --> 00:16:32,129
Forget music,
I want to dance!

406
00:16:32,163 --> 00:16:33,130
You like music?

407
00:16:33,164 --> 00:16:34,545
Sure sounds like fun,

408
00:16:34,579 --> 00:16:37,962
and I'd like to have some
of it, too, if I can.

409
00:16:37,997 --> 00:16:39,205
Don't touch me!

410
00:16:39,239 --> 00:16:40,275
That's the first big
step, boy, to want it.

411
00:16:40,309 --> 00:16:44,486
[new wave music plays]

412
00:16:46,591 --> 00:16:48,697
My boy was all
wound up, he was.

413
00:16:48,731 --> 00:16:49,594
Your boy?

414
00:16:49,629 --> 00:16:50,906
Told them all the
bandmaster told him

415
00:16:50,940 --> 00:16:52,908
how a boy could learn
discipline and coordination

416
00:16:52,942 --> 00:16:54,910
- and confidence through music.
- Oh.

417
00:16:54,944 --> 00:16:57,533
And make a personality grow.

418
00:16:57,568 --> 00:17:00,191
He said my face was
practically made for trumpet.

419
00:17:00,226 --> 00:17:01,882
I wanna learn, Dad.

420
00:17:01,917 --> 00:17:04,885
I wanna wear leotards and
a Peter Pan hat, Mom, Dad?

421
00:17:04,920 --> 00:17:07,819
Seems like you really
do a dance, son [laughs].

422
00:17:07,854 --> 00:17:09,476
If you don't think
there's a family

423
00:17:09,511 --> 00:17:11,651
- headed for the music store--
- I think it's David Bowie.

424
00:17:11,685 --> 00:17:13,342
then you don't know
parents who understand

425
00:17:13,377 --> 00:17:16,587
what it takes to
make a child happy.

426
00:17:16,621 --> 00:17:18,209
You missed your
last payments, Pops.

427
00:17:18,244 --> 00:17:20,522
A half-dozen new
reeds for your saxophone.

428
00:17:20,556 --> 00:17:22,213
By the way, how are you doing

429
00:17:22,248 --> 00:17:24,077
in the fifth-grade
band in school?

430
00:17:24,112 --> 00:17:26,528
Oh, swell. I got first chair.

431
00:17:26,562 --> 00:17:28,840
Congratulations,
that's fine, young lady.

432
00:17:28,875 --> 00:17:30,221
- Goodbye.
- Bye.

433
00:17:30,256 --> 00:17:32,154
Don't patronize me, old man.

434
00:17:32,189 --> 00:17:33,155
Hello--

435
00:17:33,190 --> 00:17:34,398
We want to ask
you some questions.

436
00:17:34,432 --> 00:17:36,158
Yes, I believe so.
The boy here'd like

437
00:17:36,193 --> 00:17:37,573
to start on the trumpet.

438
00:17:37,608 --> 00:17:39,437
Well, you're joining a
mighty happy family, young man.

439
00:17:39,472 --> 00:17:40,680
I could use one.

440
00:17:40,714 --> 00:17:42,026
My son wouldn't like
my bringing this up,

441
00:17:42,061 --> 00:17:44,270
but I imagine a good
trumpet'd put quite a dent

442
00:17:44,304 --> 00:17:46,065
in his dad's checkbook, huh?

443
00:17:46,099 --> 00:17:46,927
Yeah, if he starts
hitting you with it [laughs].

444
00:17:46,962 --> 00:17:48,343
Not necessarily.

445
00:17:48,377 --> 00:17:51,242
All the better music dealers
have a trial purchase plan.

446
00:17:51,277 --> 00:17:53,900
You are simply making
a small investment

447
00:17:53,934 --> 00:17:56,040
in your son's
lifetime personality.

448
00:17:56,075 --> 00:17:57,938
Nah, put away the grease gun
and show me something cheap.

449
00:17:57,973 --> 00:18:00,838
And with this boy's
enthusiasm, he can't miss.

450
00:18:00,872 --> 00:18:02,391
All right, let's
look at some horns.

451
00:18:02,426 --> 00:18:04,462
Hey, this is the
plot from Golden Boy.

452
00:18:04,497 --> 00:18:06,050
Yeah, where's Bill Holden?

453
00:18:06,085 --> 00:18:08,432
This trumpet is made by the
world's largest manufacturer.

454
00:18:08,466 --> 00:18:09,881
Ah, cut to the
chase. How much?

455
00:18:09,916 --> 00:18:12,574
Oh, I don't think the
make is important, is it?

456
00:18:12,608 --> 00:18:13,575
Buzz?

457
00:18:13,609 --> 00:18:14,886
I don't think the
make matters much.

458
00:18:14,921 --> 00:18:17,303
[trumpet chirps]
Doesn't matter much?!

459
00:18:17,337 --> 00:18:19,201
Mom, Dad, tell
me you heard that?

460
00:18:19,236 --> 00:18:20,478
Thought I told you, boy.

461
00:18:20,513 --> 00:18:22,791
The most fun comes from
the most skilled playing.

462
00:18:22,825 --> 00:18:25,034
And the most skill comes
only from an instrument

463
00:18:25,069 --> 00:18:27,554
designed to give you
the best sound possible.

464
00:18:27,589 --> 00:18:29,487
It sure does matter.

465
00:18:29,522 --> 00:18:30,764
Oh, excuse me,
sexless man-woman.

466
00:18:30,799 --> 00:18:32,456
Why you should see
what care goes into

467
00:18:32,490 --> 00:18:34,389
making a really
superior instrument.

468
00:18:34,423 --> 00:18:36,598
You gotta inspect
your horn, boy.

469
00:18:36,632 --> 00:18:37,771
And wash it everyday.

470
00:18:37,806 --> 00:18:39,152
You oughta take
a look at a factory

471
00:18:39,187 --> 00:18:41,154
that makes top-quality
instruments.

472
00:18:41,189 --> 00:18:42,293
Oh, may I?

473
00:18:42,328 --> 00:18:43,501
Let me tell
you what goes into

474
00:18:43,536 --> 00:18:45,848
the making of the
best instruments.

475
00:18:45,883 --> 00:18:48,851
You see? The dealer
has the same idea.

476
00:18:48,886 --> 00:18:52,372
- You listen to him, Buzz.
- Or I'll kill you.

477
00:18:52,407 --> 00:18:53,960
The instrument
with the best sound

478
00:18:53,994 --> 00:18:56,756
comes out of plans that
combine modern equipment

479
00:18:56,790 --> 00:18:58,275
with precision craftsmanship.

480
00:18:58,309 --> 00:18:59,931
Oh cripes. Polish.
That's all I do all day.

481
00:18:59,966 --> 00:19:01,761
Hmm, no noise there.
How does this thing work?

482
00:19:01,795 --> 00:19:04,522
Superior workmanship
like this is possible

483
00:19:04,557 --> 00:19:07,215
because no one man
builds an instrument.

484
00:19:07,249 --> 00:19:08,526
These'll make
lovely noises someday.

485
00:19:08,561 --> 00:19:11,943
And each
operation's performed

486
00:19:11,978 --> 00:19:15,464
- by an expert in that phase.
- Work faster, Grandma.

487
00:19:15,499 --> 00:19:16,810
[tuba music]

488
00:19:16,845 --> 00:19:21,125
This man does things
he's not proud of.

489
00:19:21,160 --> 00:19:22,195
If that craftsman
stops spinning

490
00:19:22,230 --> 00:19:24,818
that sousaphone
bell for a second,

491
00:19:24,853 --> 00:19:27,856
he would completely change
the quality of the instrument.

492
00:19:27,890 --> 00:19:30,583
The faces of those he's
wronged float up at him.

493
00:19:30,617 --> 00:19:31,756
Hey, it's Leo Gorcey.
All grown up.

494
00:19:31,791 --> 00:19:33,724
A manufacturer of
quality instruments

495
00:19:33,758 --> 00:19:36,140
