All the candy.

743
00:29:13,510 --> 00:29:15,926
Where was it taken from?

744
00:29:15,961 --> 00:29:17,825
That's a hard
question to answer.

745
00:29:17,859 --> 00:29:19,033
Look, here's what happened.

746
00:29:19,067 --> 00:29:20,344
The season opens next week and

747
00:29:20,379 --> 00:29:22,070
I'm expecting a crowd in town.

748
00:29:22,105 --> 00:29:23,071
So you were expec-- [laughs]

749
00:29:23,106 --> 00:29:24,555
So I was stocking
up on supplies,

750
00:29:24,590 --> 00:29:26,281
bringing this truckload of
groceries down from Calexico.

751
00:29:26,316 --> 00:29:27,075
- What kind of--
- Get the picture?

752
00:29:27,110 --> 00:29:28,283
Uh, yes. But--

753
00:29:28,318 --> 00:29:29,491
Well, I'd left the
club station wagon

754
00:29:29,526 --> 00:29:31,390
in San Felipe, get a
new rear end put in it.

755
00:29:31,424 --> 00:29:32,909
Oh, did you ever think of a--

756
00:29:32,943 --> 00:29:34,358
So when I got there, I had
to stop and pick that up too.

757
00:29:34,393 --> 00:29:35,359
Get the picture?

758
00:29:35,394 --> 00:29:37,085
Now, I couldn't run two cars,

759
00:29:37,120 --> 00:29:39,467
so I hired this kid
to drive the truck.

760
00:29:39,501 --> 00:29:42,297
Started him out a couple,
three hours ahead of me.

761
00:29:42,332 --> 00:29:43,574
Yeah?

762
00:29:43,609 --> 00:29:44,679
Followed him in the station
wagon. Get the picture?

763
00:29:44,713 --> 00:29:45,818
Oh sure I do, but it was--

764
00:29:45,853 --> 00:29:47,061
Well, he was supposed
to meet me here.

765
00:29:47,095 --> 00:29:49,028
He hasn't arrived yet
and that was yesterday.

766
00:29:49,063 --> 00:29:50,167
Well that would
mean he's in--

767
00:29:50,202 --> 00:29:52,031
- Have you looked for him?
- Everywhere.

768
00:29:52,066 --> 00:29:53,688
Then he must have
gone back to the club.

769
00:29:53,722 --> 00:29:55,517
Well, I just came from there.

770
00:29:55,552 --> 00:29:57,002
Then you must
have passed him up

771
00:29:57,036 --> 00:29:59,280
somewhere on the
road from San Felipe.

772
00:29:59,314 --> 00:30:00,453
If I passed him on the road,

773
00:30:00,488 --> 00:30:01,903
don't you think I'd
know my own truck?

774
00:30:01,938 --> 00:30:02,904
Get your greasy
white paws off my desk.

775
00:30:02,939 --> 00:30:04,009
I'm sure you would, señor.

776
00:30:04,043 --> 00:30:05,493
But, what if it
went off the road?

777
00:30:05,527 --> 00:30:07,081
There's no place he
can pull off the road

778
00:30:07,115 --> 00:30:08,289
without getting
stuck in the sand!

779
00:30:08,323 --> 00:30:10,325
And how could
your truck be lost?

780
00:30:10,360 --> 00:30:12,638
If it wasn't lost,
would I be coming to you?

781
00:30:12,672 --> 00:30:13,432
[all] Third base!

782
00:30:13,466 --> 00:30:15,089
That is a good
question señor.

783
00:30:15,123 --> 00:30:19,645
Tell me about this boy that was
driving it. What's his name?

784
00:30:19,679 --> 00:30:22,613
Miguel something or other.

785
00:30:22,648 --> 00:30:26,963
Probably 15, 16 years
old, dark, thin,

786
00:30:26,997 --> 00:30:28,550
about, oh about so high.

787
00:30:28,585 --> 00:30:30,000
Well señor, you
just described me.

788
00:30:30,035 --> 00:30:31,381
Would you know
him if you saw him?

789
00:30:31,415 --> 00:30:32,416
Yes!

790
00:30:32,451 --> 00:30:33,832
Come across the
street with me señor.

791
00:30:33,866 --> 00:30:35,799
We'll buy you a new boy.

792
00:30:38,353 --> 00:30:40,977
Pat Boone's going
through the liquor cabinet.

793
00:30:41,011 --> 00:30:44,152
This is stupid,
there's no F in morphine.

794
00:30:44,187 --> 00:30:47,673
Now let's see here,
little crystal methedrine,

795
00:30:47,707 --> 00:30:48,708
and that's some monoxo--

796
00:30:48,743 --> 00:30:52,091
Oh no, I was just
cleaning, just dusting.

797
00:30:52,126 --> 00:30:54,922
That's him.
Where's my truck?!

798
00:30:54,956 --> 00:30:56,199
- Here, let me.
- Uno momento.

799
00:30:56,233 --> 00:30:58,028
- What's a matter with him?
- Where's his truck?

800
00:30:58,063 --> 00:31:02,343
[speaking in Spanish]

801
00:31:05,587 --> 00:31:06,865
Did you get that?

802
00:31:06,899 --> 00:31:08,694
Miguel is a very sick boy.
He's suffering from shock.

803
00:31:08,728 --> 00:31:10,040
What from?

804
00:31:10,075 --> 00:31:13,526
[speaking in Spanish]

805
00:31:13,561 --> 00:31:14,355
He said something
shocked him.

806
00:31:14,389 --> 00:31:15,804
Nobody seems to know.

807
00:31:15,839 --> 00:31:17,737
What did you
do with my truck?!

808
00:31:17,772 --> 00:31:20,119
Here, let me. What did
you do with his truck?!

809
00:31:20,154 --> 00:31:24,537
[speaking in Spanish]

810
00:31:28,783 --> 00:31:31,820
Quiet, I'm seeing Bernadette.

811
00:31:31,855 --> 00:31:36,446
Yes? Yes, yes? He says,
"Get your elbow off my chest."

812
00:31:38,103 --> 00:31:40,726
Beware the dwarf.

813
00:31:41,761 --> 00:31:43,039
Halitosis.

814
00:31:43,073 --> 00:31:46,214
[speaking in Spanish]

815
00:31:46,249 --> 00:31:48,699
- What'd he say?
- It's no use.

816
00:31:48,734 --> 00:31:51,150
If he gets any better, the
doctor will let us know.

817
00:31:51,185 --> 00:31:52,255
Come with me, I will take you

818
00:31:52,289 --> 00:31:53,463
to the place where he was found.

819
00:31:53,497 --> 00:31:54,740
We could pump his stomach,

820
00:31:54,774 --> 00:31:57,777
but I don't think
we'd find your truck.

821
00:31:57,812 --> 00:31:59,262
Will young Miguel be cured?

822
00:31:59,296 --> 00:32:02,748
And what about the pick-up
truck? Tune in tomorrow.

823
00:32:02,782 --> 00:32:05,785
Yes, you'll drive to the
mutated monster scenes in style

824
00:32:05,820 --> 00:32:08,098
in the new 1953 Desoto.

825
00:32:08,133 --> 00:32:11,930
[ominous orchestra music]

826
00:32:13,345 --> 00:32:15,830
Would you like to have
lunch with me here, señor?

827
00:32:15,864 --> 00:32:17,452
A little pic-a-nic?

828
00:32:17,487 --> 00:32:19,903
He asked me. He asked me.

829
00:32:21,456 --> 00:32:25,322
Well, it looks like
a ton of chili to me.

830
00:32:25,357 --> 00:32:28,118
Was it your truck
made these tracks?

831
00:32:28,153 --> 00:32:29,809
No, it was beauty
killed the beast.

832
00:32:29,844 --> 00:32:34,159
Must have. I had two new
retreads like that on the rear.

833
00:32:34,193 --> 00:32:36,955
Came down the
road and got stuck.

834
00:32:36,989 --> 00:32:38,680
Yeah. Then where'd it go?

835
00:32:38,715 --> 00:32:40,268
Straight up into
the air, it looks like.

836
00:32:40,303 --> 00:32:41,442
Look, that truck had a radio

837
00:32:41,476 --> 00:32:43,237
and a heater, but it
didn't have wings.

838
00:32:43,271 --> 00:32:44,479
What's the Spanish
word for bite me?

839
00:32:44,514 --> 00:32:45,756
Now are you gonna
find it or aren't you?

840
00:32:45,791 --> 00:32:48,552
Look señor. I am just
a country policeman.

841
00:32:48,587 --> 00:32:50,382
I am paid to keep the peace.

842
00:32:50,416 --> 00:32:52,384
If someone steals, I
try to catch the thief

843
00:32:52,418 --> 00:32:55,594
and put 'em in jail,
like it says in the book.

844
00:32:55,628 --> 00:32:58,977
But about this
señor, I don't know.

845
00:32:59,011 --> 00:33:01,669
The book doesn't say anything
about a thing like this.

846
00:33:01,703 --> 00:33:04,948
Well, we're writing
the book right now, pal.

847
00:33:04,983 --> 00:33:08,262
Hey, let's drag Clarence
Thomas' name through there.

848
00:33:08,296 --> 00:33:09,988
Noise pollution.

849
00:33:10,022 --> 00:33:13,750
By 1970, this could be our
nation's number-one problem.

850
00:33:13,784 --> 00:33:15,269
Guests of the
amazing, Colossal Beast

851
00:33:15,303 --> 00:33:17,443
stay at the Beverly Hilton.

852
00:33:19,721 --> 00:33:23,139
And that's the latest report
on the international scene.

853
00:33:23,173 --> 00:33:24,519
Now, on the lighter
side of the news.

854
00:33:24,554 --> 00:33:25,693
There was a young
man from Nantucket.

855
00:33:25,727 --> 00:33:27,005
A dispatch from
Guavas, Mexico,

856
00:33:27,039 --> 00:33:29,283
says that Mr. John Swanson
is having a little trouble

857
00:33:29,317 --> 00:33:31,768
collecting insurance
on his stolen truck.

858
00:33:31,802 --> 00:33:33,080
- What happened to it?
- Oh, she's a social drinker.

859
00:33:33,114 --> 00:33:34,253
Well according
to his claim report,

860
00:33:34,288 --> 00:33:37,567
it disappeared without
leaving any tracks.

861
00:33:37,601 --> 00:33:41,502
Mr. Swanson says something
must've carried off his truck.

862
00:33:41,536 --> 00:33:44,056
Now, what could have
carried off his truck?

863
00:33:44,091 --> 00:33:45,851
Ha-ha, that's whimsical.

864
00:33:45,885 --> 00:33:48,267
And here in Los Angeles,
the city council has decided

865
00:33:48,302 --> 00:33:49,475
to vote upon the referendum

866
00:33:49,510 --> 00:33:52,340
for increasing the
pay of teachers.

867
00:33:52,375 --> 00:33:53,859
With the interest that
has recently been aroused

868
00:33:53,893 --> 00:33:54,963
regarding the importance
of better schools--

869
00:33:54,998 --> 00:33:56,275
Hello, I'm out of tonic.

870
00:33:56,310 --> 00:33:59,796
Operator, I want to place
a person-to-person call.

871
00:33:59,830 --> 00:34:02,730
To Mr. John Swanson
in Guavos, Mexico.

872
00:34:02,764 --> 00:34:03,972
I'll hold the line.

873
00:34:04,007 --> 00:34:05,388
Uh, you are holding the line.

874
00:34:05,422 --> 00:34:06,803
I'm getting me a good lawyer.

875
00:34:06,837 --> 00:34:08,770
And if one ain't
enough, I'm getting two.

876
00:34:08,805 --> 00:34:10,082
And I'm gonna make
the insurance company

877
00:34:10,117 --> 00:34:11,601
pay up and pay up fast.

878
00:34:11,635 --> 00:34:12,740
I'm taking 'em to court by the--

879
00:34:12,774 --> 00:34:14,328
Oh, who am I
kidding? I love you.

880
00:34:14,362 --> 00:34:17,952
I'm sorry, I guess I
shouldn't blow my top like that.

881
00:34:17,986 --> 00:34:19,126
Uh, you don't have a top.

882
00:34:19,160 --> 00:34:19,954
But if somebody
calls me a liar, I--

883
00:34:19,988 --> 00:34:21,024
[doorbell rings]

884
00:34:21,059 --> 00:34:21,887
Sorry, time's up.
Thanks for playing.

885
00:34:21,921 --> 00:34:22,370
Excuse me.

886
00:34:22,405 --> 00:34:26,616
[new wave music]

887
00:34:32,277 --> 00:34:33,657
Joyce Manning?

888
00:34:33,692 --> 00:34:35,935
Yes, you must be Major
Baird. Please Come in.

889
00:34:35,970 --> 00:34:37,661
Thank you.

890
00:34:37,696 --> 00:34:39,318
Who's the stiff?

891
00:34:39,353 --> 00:34:41,182
This is Mr.
Swanson. Major Baird.

892
00:34:41,217 --> 00:34:42,252
- How do you do, sir?
- Howdy.

893
00:34:42,287 --> 00:34:43,357
Hit the road, Pops.

894
00:34:43,391 --> 00:34:45,290
- Please be seated.
- Thank you.

895
00:34:45,324 --> 00:34:46,670
Would you like a drink?

896
00:34:46,705 --> 00:34:48,707
No thanks.

897
00:34:48,741 --> 00:34:50,398
Mr. Swanson was good
enough to come up here

898
00:34:50,433 --> 00:34:52,297
and tell me of his experience.

899
00:34:52,331 --> 00:34:53,712
It was a mere nothing.

900
00:34:53,746 --> 00:34:55,231
I had to be in Los
Angeles anyway today

901
00:34:55,265 --> 00:34:56,991
to see the insurance people.

902
00:34:57,025 --> 00:34:58,061
Have I missed much?

903
00:34:58,096 --> 00:34:59,166
Well, he tells me
that he wasn't there

904
00:34:59,200 --> 00:35:00,719
to see what happened
to his truck.

905
00:35:00,753 --> 00:35:03,584
I guess I got
there just too late.

906
00:35:03,618 --> 00:35:04,792
Oh, I sense the irony.

907
00:35:04,826 --> 00:35:06,345
Besides the truck's
not being there,

908
00:35:06,380 --> 00:35:08,830
did you see anything unusual.

909
00:35:08,865 --> 00:35:11,488
- Like what?
- Tracks in the sand.

910
00:35:11,523 --> 00:35:13,387
Well, there were
car tracks, sure.

911
00:35:13,421 --> 00:35:15,354
But no animal tracks?

912
00:35:15,389 --> 00:35:16,666
Animal tracks?

913
00:35:16,700 --> 00:35:19,220
Human footprints,
Mr. Swanson.

914
00:35:19,255 --> 00:35:20,808
Well what's so
unusual about that?

915
00:35:20,842 --> 00:35:23,983
These would be the tracks
of a big man, a very big man.

916
00:35:24,018 --> 00:35:25,157
We're talkin' big.

917
00:35:25,192 --> 00:35:28,402
There were Miguel's.
I don't get the picture.

918
00:35:28,436 --> 00:35:30,058
- How big do you mean?
- Very big.

919
00:35:30,093 --> 00:35:30,956
Dom DeLuise big?

920
00:35:30,990 --> 00:35:32,647
10 times as big as you.

921
00:35:32,682 --> 00:35:34,373
Are you trying to kid me?

922
00:35:34,408 --> 00:35:36,030
Hey, I'm drunk,
but not that drunk!

923
00:35:36,064 --> 00:35:37,894
Not at all. I'm trying
to find my brother.

924
00:35:37,928 --> 00:35:39,654
He got a bad dose
of radiation poisoning

925
00:35:39,689 --> 00:35:42,001
in the course of testing
a plutonium bomb.

926
00:35:42,036 --> 00:35:45,212
He started growing at the rate
of eight to ten feet a day.

927
00:35:45,246 --> 00:35:46,178
He blew his
mind out in a car.

928
00:35:46,213 --> 00:35:47,593
You may have read about him.

929
00:35:47,628 --> 00:35:50,976
Sure, Colonel Glenn
Manning, the Colossal Man.

930
00:35:51,010 --> 00:35:52,046
I remember.

931
00:35:52,080 --> 00:35:53,185
Glenn had reached a height

932
00:35:53,220 --> 00:35:55,222
of over 60 feet
when he disappeared.

933
00:35:55,256 --> 00:35:56,188
I'll have what she's having.

934
00:35:56,223 --> 00:35:57,362
Didn't he get
shot and fall off

935
00:35:57,396 --> 00:35:58,880
of Boulder Dam and get killed?

936
00:35:58,915 --> 00:36:01,538
That's right. But Miss
Manning seems to have an idea

937
00:36:01,573 --> 00:36:04,369
that he survived both the
artillery fire and the fall.

938
00:36:04,403 --> 00:36:05,646
You see, she lives in denial.

939
00:36:05,680 --> 00:36:07,199
That is what you're
getting at, isn't it?

940
00:36:07,234 --> 00:36:08,994
I'll leave it
up to Mr. Swanson.

941
00:36:09,028 --> 00:36:11,583
Wouldn't that account for the
disappearance of your truck?

942
00:36:11,617 --> 00:36:13,930
Look lady, leave
me out of this.

943
00:36:13,964 --> 00:36:17,071
I didn't see no footprints.
I didn't see no giant.

944
00:36:17,105 --> 00:36:18,383
I didn't see anything.

945
00:36:18,417 --> 00:36:21,179
I've got enough trouble with
the insurance company as it is.

946
00:36:21,213 --> 00:36:22,249
Get the picture?

947
00:36:22,283 --> 00:36:24,112
I've had enough
hurt in my life.

948
00:36:24,147 --> 00:36:27,012
Can you think of anyone
else who might help me?

949
00:36:27,046 --> 00:36:28,669
There's a boy named
Miguel down there,

950
00:36:28,703 --> 00:36:30,464
but he's not doing any talking.

951
00:36:30,498 --> 00:36:33,018
Doctor said he's suffering
from shock or something.

952
00:36:33,052 --> 00:36:35,952
I'm sorry I can't do you
more good Miss Manning.

953
00:36:35,986 --> 00:36:37,885
Thank you for
coming, Mr. Swanson.

954
00:36:37,919 --> 00:36:40,681
Oh say, can I have a
to-go cup for that gin?

955
00:36:42,752 --> 00:36:45,133
Hadn't you better give
him up, Miss Manning?

956
00:36:45,168 --> 00:36:46,514
The Army did some time ago.

957
00:36:46,549 --> 00:36:47,895
You never found his body.

958
00:36:47,929 --> 00:36:50,829
The river below the dam is
a mile deep in some places.

959
00:36:50,863 --> 00:36:54,867
His body's down there somewhere
and it'll show up in time.

960
00:36:54,902 --> 00:36:57,698
You think Glenn made his
way down that river somehow

961
00:36:57,732 --> 00:36:59,458
and reached the Gulf of
California, don't you?

962
00:36:59,493 --> 00:37:00,390
The whole sequel
depends on it!

963
00:37:00,425 --> 00:37:02,047
Believe me, it's impossible.

964
00:37:02,081 --> 00:37:04,843
I was in charge of the
search for him and I know.

965
00:37:04,877 --> 00:37:05,947
The medical
authorities all agree.

966
00:37:05,982 --> 00:37:07,811
No man, no matter
what his size--

967
00:37:07,846 --> 00:37:08,847
Is an island?

968
00:37:08,881 --> 00:37:10,158
could take those
two bazooka charges

969
00:37:10,193 --> 00:37:11,608
and a drop of over 700 feet--

970
00:37:11,643 --> 00:37:12,747
And not want it every night.

971
00:37:12,782 --> 00:37:15,163
and come through it alive.

972
00:37:15,198 --> 00:37:16,855
Well, those are the facts.

973
00:37:16,889 --> 00:37:20,168
Your brother died a
watery death. Well, gotta go.

974
00:37:20,203 --> 00:37:21,791
Take my advice and face them.

975
00:37:21,825 --> 00:37:25,035
I shall, Major.
Thank you for coming.

976
00:37:25,070 --> 00:37:27,175
I'm afraid it wasn't
worth your while.

977
00:37:27,210 --> 00:37:29,247
It could be if you'd have
dinner with me this evening.

978
00:37:29,281 --> 00:37:30,144
Woah, down boy!

979
00:37:30,178 --> 00:37:31,663
I'd like to, but I can't.

980
00:37:31,697 --> 00:37:34,182
I'm leaving for
Mexico right away.

981
00:37:34,217 --> 00:37:35,425
- Goodbye.
- Oh man, I blew it.

982
00:37:35,460 --> 00:37:38,842
- Goodbye.
- Stupid, stupid, stupid.

983
00:37:38,877 --> 00:37:40,741
What a dink.

984
00:37:42,777 --> 00:37:46,229
Life is kind of different
in Grover's Corneros.

985
00:37:46,264 --> 00:37:47,126
[slow Latin music]

986
00:37:47,161 --> 00:37:48,473
It could be worth my while

987
00:37:48,507 --> 00:37:50,889
if you had dinner
with me tonight.

988
00:37:52,235 --> 00:37:54,133
Oh, just snap out of it.

989
00:37:54,168 --> 00:37:56,135
That is the boy, Miguel.

990
00:37:56,170 --> 00:37:57,551
Has he told you anything yet?

991
00:37:57,585 --> 00:38:00,381
No. But you can
question him if you like.

992
00:38:00,416 --> 00:38:01,934
He's supposed to speak
a little English.

993
00:38:01,969 --> 00:38:03,108
At least, that's
what the résumé said.

994
00:38:03,142 --> 00:38:06,560
- Miguel? Miguel?
- His name is Moisha.

995
00:38:06,594 --> 00:38:08,044
You see, señorita?

996
00:38:08,078 --> 00:38:09,183
You better come with me.

997
00:38:09,217 --> 00:38:10,874
I will find a place for
you to spend the night.

998
00:38:10,909 --> 00:38:11,875
I know.

999
00:38:11,910 --> 00:38:13,187
Thanks, but I'm
going to stay here.

1000
00:38:13,221 --> 00:38:15,189
All night?

1001
00:38:15,223 --> 00:38:17,191
As long as necessary.

1002
00:38:17,225 --> 00:38:19,366
Then my wife better
get you something to eat.

1003
00:38:19,400 --> 00:38:20,919
Thank you.

1004
00:38:22,507 --> 00:38:26,062
Okay Miguel, move
over. I'm hopping in.

1005
00:38:26,096 --> 00:38:29,134
He said dark, and then he
said good, and then he died.

1006
00:38:29,168 --> 00:38:33,207
[gasping]

1007
00:38:33,241 --> 00:38:37,073
Swim, run, huge.
Dean, no, stop.

1008
00:38:37,107 --> 00:38:38,833
What is it Miguel?

1009
00:38:38,868 --> 00:38:43,493
[grunting]
First word, sounds like...

1010
00:38:43,528 --> 00:38:44,839
Hey, he sounds like Eeyore.

1011
00:38:44,874 --> 00:38:46,082
Seargant!

1012
00:38:46,116 --> 00:38:48,636
Could you shut him
up, I'm trying to sleep?

1013
00:38:49,913 --> 00:38:51,743
Get in here,
this is hysterical.

1014
00:38:51,777 --> 00:38:54,815
[grunting continues]

1015
00:38:54,849 --> 00:38:58,128
What now? Jeez. Can't
leave you two alone?

1016
00:39:02,374 --> 00:39:05,066
Oil can? Oil can?

1017
00:39:10,520 --> 00:39:12,108
He's crying for his mother.

1018
00:39:12,142 --> 00:39:14,179
Her name was Uuugh.

1019
00:39:14,213 --> 00:39:18,425
I do not understand what
he's trying to say señorita.

1020
00:39:18,459 --> 00:39:20,979
Call me if he speaks again.

1021
00:39:21,013 --> 00:39:23,050
[screams loudly]
He spoke again!

1022
00:39:23,084 --> 00:39:25,984
Ogro! Ogro!

1023
00:39:26,018 --> 00:39:28,573
Oh, I get it. He's
channeling Frances Farmer.

1024
00:39:28,607 --> 00:39:29,988
Miguel.

1025
00:39:30,022 --> 00:39:32,059
Shouldn't eat chili
peppers, they burned his gut.

1026
00:39:32,093 --> 00:39:35,131
Miguel. Miguel.

1027
00:39:35,165 --> 00:39:38,962
I had Jell-o today.

1028
00:39:38,997 --> 00:39:40,170
All right, you got the part.

1029
00:39:40,205 --> 00:39:41,655
What did he mean?

1030
00:39:41,689 --> 00:39:43,035
It's hard to say.

1031
00:39:43,070 --> 00:39:44,071
It's a guy thing.

1032
00:39:44,105 --> 00:39:46,142
Ogro, what is that?

1033
00:39:46,176 --> 00:39:51,803
It's a big fellow, like an
ogre in a story, a monster.

1034
00:39:51,837 --> 00:39:52,976
A giant.

1035
00:39:53,011 --> 00:39:55,116
He must have been dreaming.

1036
00:39:55,151 --> 00:39:57,602
Was it a giant, Miguel?

1037
00:39:57,636 --> 00:39:59,707
- Was it a giant man?
- Did it make you mad?

1038
00:39:59,742 --> 00:40:02,123
- Did it make you mean mad?
- Was it, Miguel?

1039
00:40:02,158 --> 00:40:04,850
[classical music]

1040
00:40:04,885 --> 00:40:07,128
Might have better
luck when he's awake.

1041
00:40:07,163 --> 00:40:12,099
That's our boy, we'll
be back after these words.

1042
00:40:12,133 --> 00:40:14,757
I remember this scene.

1043
00:40:14,791 --> 00:40:16,034
Hey, it's the guy
that was standing

1044
00:40:16,068 --> 00:40:17,449
next to Oswald when he got shot.

1045
00:40:17,484 --> 00:40:18,933
I just wanted to let you
know, I got your phone call.

1046
00:40:18,968 --> 00:40:20,452
This is Dr. Carmichael.

1047
00:40:20,487 --> 00:40:21,798
He's head of our
radiation excursion.

1048
00:40:21,833 --> 00:40:23,075
Hello, Dr. Carmichael.

1049
00:40:23,110 --> 00:40:24,491
He's very much interested
in your brother's case.

1050
00:40:24,525 --> 00:40:26,147
I only hope I can do
something for him, Ms. Manning.

1051
00:40:26,182 --> 00:40:27,701
Thank you, Doctor.

1052
00:40:27,735 --> 00:40:29,427
Doctor Carmichael,
Major Baird,

1053
00:40:29,461 --> 00:40:31,946
this is Sergeant Mario of
the Mexican State Police.

1054
00:40:31,981 --> 00:40:33,085
Hi.

1055
00:40:33,120 --> 00:40:34,915
Where was it the boy saw
the Colossal Man, the giant?

1056
00:40:34,949 --> 00:40:38,021
El Ogro? He has not
been able to tell us.

1057
00:40:38,056 --> 00:40:38,988
The sergeant
can show you where

1058
00:40:39,022 --> 00:40:40,576
the truck disappeared though.

1059
00:40:40,610 --> 00:40:43,130
Si, señor. Won't you
get in my automobile?

1060
00:40:43,164 --> 00:40:44,062
Fine.

1061
00:40:44,096 --> 00:40:46,271
Fine, but I don't
want to ride in back,

1062
00:40:46,305 --> 00:40:49,101
I've always got to ride in back.

1063
00:40:51,932 --> 00:40:55,280
[all sing] 100 years of
solitude, 100 years of solitude,

1064
00:40:55,314 --> 00:41:00,906
take one down, pass it
around, 99 years of solitude.

1065
00:41:00,941 --> 00:41:03,184
These tracks were made
by the truck he was driving.

1066
00:41:03,219 --> 00:41:07,257
The truck apparently
skidded into the water,

1067
00:41:07,292 --> 00:41:08,914
and never drove out.

1068
00:41:08,949 --> 00:41:10,675
Thank you, Mr. Obvious.

1069
00:41:10,709 --> 00:41:11,710
What do you think, Mark?

1070
00:41:11,745 --> 00:41:12,884
What did happen to Oswald?

1071
00:41:12,918 --> 00:41:14,230
Well, it seems like
pretty meager evidence

1072
00:41:14,264 --> 00:41:16,197
to conclude because
a truck disappeared,

1073
00:41:16,232 --> 00:41:17,751
that the Colossal
Man is still alive.

1074
00:41:17,785 --> 00:41:19,028
Oh, my god. Her arms!
Oh, it's her sweater.

1075
00:41:19,062 --> 00:41:20,029
But the boy?

1076
00:41:20,063 --> 00:41:22,065
I'm sure there's some
logical explanation.

1077
00:41:22,100 --> 00:41:27,381
Señores, señores,
come quickly!

1078
00:41:27,415 --> 00:41:30,384
Look at these cute tadpoles.

1079
00:41:31,696 --> 00:41:34,008
That is a very
big foot print.

1080
00:41:34,043 --> 00:41:36,839
Major Baird, is that
enough logic for you?

1081
00:41:36,873 --> 00:41:39,117
The foot that made
that print is about

1082
00:41:39,151 --> 00:41:42,051
30 times the size
of a normal man.

1083
00:41:42,085 --> 00:41:44,432
That would make him
about 60 feet tall.

1084
00:41:44,467 --> 00:41:45,986
Glenn was 60 feet tall.

1085
00:41:46,020 --> 00:41:47,125
Think there's a connection?

1086
00:41:47,159 --> 00:41:48,402
Nah.

1087
00:41:48,436 --> 00:41:50,335
He must have gone that
way, to the mountains.

1088
00:41:50,369 --> 00:41:53,338
To the prairie, to the
oceans white with foam.

1089
00:41:53,372 --> 00:41:57,169
[ominous classical music]

1090
00:41:57,204 --> 00:41:58,895
He always summer's
in the mountains.

1091
00:41:58,930 --> 00:42:01,829
He stays at the beautiful
Mount Airy Lodge.

1092
00:42:03,141 --> 00:42:05,074
You think he could be
up in those mountains?

1093
00:42:05,108 --> 00:42:07,870
Yes, it could be. There
are no people up there.

1094
00:42:07,904 --> 00:42:09,699
Well, let's drive slowly.

1095
00:42:09,734 --> 00:42:11,908
Maybe we could find
some more foot prints.

1096
00:42:11,943 --> 00:42:13,876
Hey, think there's some
place to eat around here?

1097
00:42:13,910 --> 00:42:15,878
I could really go for a
Jack in the Box right now.

1098
00:42:15,912 --> 00:42:17,086
Yeah, that'd be good.

1099
00:42:17,120 --> 00:42:19,606
We're gonna get a
four-door next time.

1100
00:42:19,640 --> 00:42:21,918
Get one of those
breakfast muffins.

1101
00:42:21,953 --> 00:42:23,782
- Just double clutch.
- Really?

1102
00:42:23,817 --> 00:42:25,370
Just get it out of there.

1103
00:42:25,404 --> 00:42:27,890
Just grind it and find it.

1104
00:42:27,924 --> 00:42:29,719
That's right, spare
us nothing Burt.

1105
00:42:29,754 --> 00:42:30,996
Let's get out of here.

1106
00:42:31,031 --> 00:42:34,931
Three Jacks and a Jill
and three Jills in a Jeep.

1107
00:42:34,966 --> 00:42:37,278
On American Classics.

1108
00:42:39,591 --> 00:42:44,423
[new wave music]

1109
00:42:45,459 --> 00:42:48,220
Who's that guy
with the big head?

1110
00:42:48,255 --> 00:42:49,670
Big head.

1111
00:42:49,705 --> 00:42:50,982
Big head.

1112
00:42:51,016 --> 00:42:54,088
Who's that guy with
the big, big head?

1113
00:42:54,123 --> 00:42:55,849
- Big head.
- Big head.

1114
00:42:55,883 --> 00:42:58,265
Big head.
[all] Big head! Yeah! Whoo!

1115
00:42:58,299 --> 00:43:00,923
OK, that was pretty good,
but let's try it again,

1116
00:43:00,957 --> 00:43:02,234
and let's try to
stay in the same key

1117
00:43:02,269 --> 00:43:03,926
and begin at the same time, OK?

1118
00:43:03,960 --> 00:43:05,721
All right, yeah.

1119
00:43:05,755 --> 00:43:06,929
A one, a two, a one two--

1120
00:43:06,963 --> 00:43:08,896
[loud banging]

1121
00:43:08,931 --> 00:43:09,725
Oh, let's try it again.

1122
00:43:09,759 --> 00:43:11,519
OK.

1123
00:43:11,554 --> 00:43:13,142
A one, a two, a one two--

1124
00:43:13,176 --> 00:43:14,488
[loud banging]

1125
00:43:14,522 --> 00:43:15,765
Crow, would you cut that out?

1126
00:43:15,800 --> 00:43:16,939
I'm not doin' anything.

1127
00:43:16,973 --> 00:43:18,665
[loud banging]

1128
00:43:18,699 --> 00:43:19,838
Joel, it's not
us, it sounds like

1129
00:43:19,873 --> 00:43:21,219
it's coming from
outside the ship.

1130
00:43:21,253 --> 00:43:22,876
Hello?

1131
00:43:22,910 --> 00:43:24,118
- Anybody home?
- Oh, my goodness.

1132
00:43:24,153 --> 00:43:25,948
Hey Cambot, give me
rocket number nine, quick.

1133
00:43:25,982 --> 00:43:28,157
I want to see the
exterior of the ship.

1134
00:43:28,191 --> 00:43:32,126
Oh, oh there you
are. I heard music.

1135
00:43:32,161 --> 00:43:35,233
You weren't talking
about me, were you?

1136
00:43:35,267 --> 00:43:37,615
Ditch the head.

1137
00:43:37,649 --> 00:43:39,444
Hi, Glenn, good
to see you again.

1138
00:43:39,478 --> 00:43:40,997
How you been, buddy?

1139
00:43:41,032 --> 00:43:42,171
Yeah, did you ever find out

1140
00:43:42,205 --> 00:43:44,725
about that whole
share-rib thing?

1141
00:43:44,760 --> 00:43:46,175
Hey, Crow come
on take it easy.

1142
00:43:46,209 --> 00:43:49,799
Mr. Manning doesn't have time
for your little shenanigans.

1143
00:43:49,834 --> 00:43:51,111
Hey, you know what?

1144
00:43:51,145 --> 00:43:54,321
We were just watching an
experiment about you, Glenn.

1145
00:43:54,355 --> 00:43:58,497
[chewing] I'm sorry.

1146
00:44:00,016 --> 00:44:02,985
I walked all the way over from
the other side of the planet.

1147
00:44:03,019 --> 00:44:06,574
You know, I've been
picking up transmissions

1148
00:44:06,609 --> 00:44:09,577
of your experiments since
I saw you last time,

1149
00:44:09,612 --> 00:44:11,959
I had a whole head full
of goofy dental work.

1150
00:44:11,994 --> 00:44:13,098
You know, I'm a
little torqued off

1151
00:44:13,133 --> 00:44:15,963
about this whole
cheesy sequel thing.

1152
00:44:15,998 --> 00:44:18,656
Yeah, we're all wondering
why you weren't in this one.

1153
00:44:18,690 --> 00:44:20,968
That's what I'm
goin' on about.

1154
00:44:21,003 --> 00:44:22,556
You know, I create this role,

1155
00:44:22,590 --> 00:44:24,006
do this work in the first film.

1156
00:44:24,040 --> 00:44:25,939
Everybody loved my work.

1157
00:44:25,973 --> 00:44:27,388
Excuse me.

1158
00:44:27,423 --> 00:44:29,459
And then, you know
everything's going great.

1159
00:44:29,494 --> 00:44:31,116
Burt and I are good
friends, and then

1160
00:44:31,151 --> 00:44:35,880
the studio's gonna do a sequel.

1161
00:44:35,914 --> 00:44:38,537
I figure it's a done
deal basically, right?

1162
00:44:38,572 --> 00:44:40,678
I'm the perfect man for the job.

1163
00:44:40,712 --> 00:44:42,921
But then, the phone
stops ringing.

1164
00:44:42,956 --> 00:44:44,060
People I thought were my friends

1165
00:44:44,095 --> 00:44:46,407
don't even look me
in the eye anymore.

1166
00:44:46,442 --> 00:44:48,133
[mumbles]

1167
00:44:48,168 --> 00:44:50,411
They pulled a Roger Moore
on me is what they did.

1168
00:44:50,446 --> 00:44:54,968
I thought I was gonna be in
the film, and then nothing.

1169
00:44:55,002 --> 00:44:58,350
I'm sorry. Showbusiness.
I could go on for hours.

1170
00:44:58,385 --> 00:45:00,870
But I didn't come here
to bore, I'm sorry.

1171
00:45:00,905 --> 00:45:03,286
Well, why did you
come here big guy?

1172
00:45:03,321 --> 00:45:07,877
Oh, it was because
I had something...

1173
00:45:07,912 --> 00:45:11,605
I forgot it. I don't
have any pockets.

1174
00:45:11,639 --> 00:45:13,020
Kind of hard to--

1175
00:45:13,055 --> 00:45:16,127
Whoa, whoa. I'm gettin' kind
of a weird signal there.

1176
00:45:16,161 --> 00:45:18,785
It's probably comin'
off your satellite dish.

1177
00:45:18,819 --> 00:45:21,201
It's Golden Girls. Yeah.

1178
00:45:21,235 --> 00:45:24,238
I'm gonna hang around outside
here if you don't mind,

1179
00:45:24,273 --> 00:45:28,035
I kinda have a thing
for Bea Arthur.

1180
00:45:28,070 --> 00:45:29,623
I'll just hang and wait--

1181
00:45:29,657 --> 00:45:32,591
[grunts loudly]

1182
00:45:32,626 --> 00:45:34,455
[all] Whoa! Ah!
[buzzers]

1183
00:45:34,490 --> 00:45:37,079
We got a movie sign. Ah!

1184
00:45:37,113 --> 00:45:42,084
[doors open hydraulically]

1185
00:45:47,123 --> 00:45:51,196
[all singing] 52 years of
solitude, 52 years of solitude,

1186
00:45:51,231 --> 00:45:54,096
Ha-ha, I like that.

1187
00:45:55,131 --> 00:45:56,305
Excuse me, I'll
just be a minute.

1188
00:45:56,339 --> 00:45:58,928
I'm cramping up here. Excuse me.

1189
00:45:58,963 --> 00:46:00,723
Must've been that changa.

1190
00:46:00,758 --> 00:46:02,035
What do you see, Louis?

1191
00:46:02,069 --> 00:46:02,932
There are no more footprints,

1192
00:46:02,967 --> 00:46:04,140
the ground is too hard here.

1193
00:46:04,175 --> 00:46:05,728
I see something over there.

1194
00:46:05,763 --> 00:46:09,490
Look! It's a big turd
with track marks in it.

1195
00:46:11,044 --> 00:46:13,115
Hey, there's a Hard
Rock Cafe over there.

1196
00:46:13,149 --> 00:46:15,255
They're everywhere now.

1197
00:46:15,289 --> 00:46:18,327
[ominous classical music]

1198
00:46:21,744 --> 00:46:24,574
Oh no, Glenn passed
a truck on the highway.

1199
00:46:24,609 --> 00:46:27,163
I mean he passed a
truck on the highway.

1200
00:46:27,198 --> 00:46:29,925
This must be part of
Swanson's missing truck.

1201
00:46:29,959 --> 00:46:31,236
No, this another
truck señorita,

1202
00:46:31,271 --> 00:46:34,964
Swanson's truck is
dark green [mumbles].

1203
00:46:34,999 --> 00:46:36,138
Yeah, but it's
bound to change color

1204
00:46:36,172 --> 00:46:38,381
after it's been
through his system.

1205
00:46:38,416 --> 00:46:40,383
Tom, will you stop with
the crap jokes please?

1206
00:46:40,418 --> 00:46:42,627
Hey, Wile E. Coyote
must be up there,

1207
00:46:42,661 --> 00:46:44,560
to change to subject.

1208
00:46:44,594 --> 00:46:46,113
Hey, want a Rolling Rock?

1209
00:46:46,148 --> 00:46:49,289
[laughs]
Kidding.

1210
00:46:49,323 --> 00:46:51,498
Nothing but a rock slide.

1211
00:46:51,532 --> 00:46:54,950
It's getting late.
We better go home.

1212
00:46:54,984 --> 00:46:57,021
It's not good to
be here after dark.

1213
00:46:57,055 --> 00:47:00,748
That's right, you
cannot trust me after dark.

1214
00:47:00,783 --> 00:47:03,096
I was gettin' tired anyway.

1215
00:47:03,130 --> 00:47:05,201
Man, it's been a big day huh?

1216
00:47:05,236 --> 00:47:06,685
What did you think
of those big rocks?

1217
00:47:06,720 --> 00:47:08,826
What were they all about anyway?

1218
00:47:08,860 --> 00:47:10,793
Oh let'’s knock off.

1219
00:47:10,828 --> 00:47:14,279
And the Grinch peered down
with his little dog, Max.

1220
00:47:14,314 --> 00:47:18,594
[slurping and burping]

1221
00:47:18,628 --> 00:47:19,181
Pardon me.

1222
00:47:19,215 --> 00:47:20,389
New York City.

1223
00:47:20,423 --> 00:47:21,355
Just pretend to
eat it and fold it

1224
00:47:21,390 --> 00:47:23,841
in your napkin and say, "Mmm".

1225
00:47:23,875 --> 00:47:25,014
Oh, no thanks.

1226
00:47:25,049 --> 00:47:25,912
Oh, no, no, no.
I'm going to get

1227
00:47:25,946 --> 00:47:29,639
the Aztec two-step as it is.

1228
00:47:29,674 --> 00:47:30,848
Sorry to be so long.

1229
00:47:30,882 --> 00:47:31,952
Bragger.

1230
00:47:31,987 --> 00:47:33,057
But I just talked
to the Mexican

1231
00:47:33,091 --> 00:47:34,230
Military Authorities
on the phone.

1232
00:47:34,265 --> 00:47:35,473
Couldn't understand a word.

1233
00:47:35,507 --> 00:47:37,095
They have troops and
artillery standing by.

1234
00:47:37,130 --> 00:47:39,339
All we have to do is
pinpoint the Colossal Man,

1235
00:47:39,373 --> 00:47:41,099
and they'll move in on him.

1236
00:47:41,134 --> 00:47:43,412
He might come willingly
if we reasoned with him.

1237
00:47:43,446 --> 00:47:44,482
Nuh-uh.

1238
00:47:44,516 --> 00:47:46,001
How do you reason
with a 60 foot giant?

1239
00:47:46,035 --> 00:47:47,140
It's possible he'd
listen to someone

1240
00:47:47,174 --> 00:47:48,935
he knows well,
like Miss Manning.

1241
00:47:48,969 --> 00:47:50,384
Ah, eat your
fajita and shut up.

1242
00:47:50,419 --> 00:47:52,317
No, I don't want
anybody getting hurt.

1243
00:47:52,352 --> 00:47:53,974
Would someone please
pass the sour cream?

1244
00:47:54,009 --> 00:47:56,080
Please let me try at least.

1245
00:47:56,114 --> 00:47:57,115
No, I'm sorry.

1246
00:47:57,150 --> 00:47:58,116
Be reasonable,
if you use force

1247
00:47:58,151 --> 00:48:00,291
on him, somebody will get hurt.

1248
00:48:00,325 --> 00:48:02,017
Nope, we're closed. Sorry.

1249
00:48:02,051 --> 00:48:03,742
Very well then.
I'll go alone.

1250
00:48:03,777 --> 00:48:04,778
And I'll force myself on him.

1251
00:48:04,812 --> 00:48:06,814
Wait. Miss Manning!

1252
00:48:08,057 --> 00:48:10,646
Miss Manning, I
didn't ask for this job,

1253
00:48:10,680 --> 00:48:11,889
I was assigned to it.

1254
00:48:11,923 --> 00:48:12,993
And if I'm going to get it done,

1255
00:48:13,028 --> 00:48:14,684
I'll have to handle
it my own way.

1256
00:48:14,719 --> 00:48:15,720
Well it seems to me your way

1257
00:48:15,754 --> 00:48:17,549
is going to hurt him
more than help him.

1258
00:48:17,584 --> 00:48:20,173
I have to think of the
safety of others as well as him.

1259
00:48:20,207 --> 00:48:21,312
That goes for you too.

1260
00:48:21,346 --> 00:48:22,761
It's my way or the highway.

1261
00:48:22,796 --> 00:48:24,832
I'm to forget he's my brother
and do nothing, I suppose?

1262
00:48:24,867 --> 00:48:26,869
It's natural for you
to be concerned about him.

1263
00:48:26,904 --> 00:48:27,732
Natural.

1264
00:48:27,766 --> 00:48:29,044
Can't you let it go at that?

1265
00:48:29,078 --> 00:48:30,666
You don't even know if
he'll recognize you or not.

1266
00:48:30,700 --> 00:48:31,770
He won't even
be able to see you.

1267
00:48:31,805 --> 00:48:33,082
Let me find out.

1268
00:48:33,117 --> 00:48:35,050
We'll see about it tomorrow
when the soldiers arrive.

1269
00:48:35,084 --> 00:48:38,777
Today please, before
they get here. Please.

1270
00:48:38,812 --> 00:48:39,813
What makes you so sure
you're going to find him?

1271
00:48:39,847 --> 00:48:41,194
He's 60 flippin' feet tall!

1272
00:48:41,228 --> 00:48:43,713
There's a whole
mountain he has to hide in.

1273
00:48:43,748 --> 00:48:48,028
We have something to
go on. Those footprints.

1274
00:48:48,063 --> 00:48:49,857
All right. Get in my car.

1275
00:48:49,892 --> 00:48:53,792
I guess the great
sister of Glenn knows best.

1276
00:48:53,827 --> 00:48:55,933
I'll drive,
scoot over. Scoot.

1277
00:48:55,967 --> 00:48:58,211
[ominous classical music]

1278
00:48:58,245 --> 00:48:59,281
You sure this is
where William Holden

1279
00:48:59,315 --> 00:49:01,628
did that scene in Picnic?

1280
00:49:01,662 --> 00:49:05,459
Hey, let's drop this whole
Glenn thing, I love you.

1281
00:49:07,565 --> 00:49:11,327
Well, they'’re in the valley
of the Jolly Green Giant.

1282
00:49:13,260 --> 00:49:16,125
Maybe they should get
Crazy Horse up on that...

1283
00:49:16,160 --> 00:49:18,162
Not enough time.

1284
00:49:18,196 --> 00:49:21,096
Why are they playing
The Last Emperor music?!

1285
00:49:21,130 --> 00:49:23,029
Beats me.

1286
00:49:23,063 --> 00:49:25,583
Classic.

1287
00:49:25,617 --> 00:49:28,896
Well look, it's the
Fred Sanford memorial.

1288
00:49:30,208 --> 00:49:31,347
A litter box.

1289
00:49:31,382 --> 00:49:32,831
"This'll be our home honey.

1290
00:49:32,866 --> 00:49:36,939
"We'll put the living room over
here, just like we dreamed."

1291
00:49:36,974 --> 00:49:40,080
Looks more like a Wisconsin
farmer's frightening lawn.

1292
00:49:40,115 --> 00:49:41,944
[laughs]

1293
00:49:45,706 --> 00:49:48,364
Similac. What's
he need that for?

1294
00:49:48,399 --> 00:49:51,091
He's been raiding these
trucks for food to live on.

1295
00:49:51,126 --> 00:49:52,748
Jerk, didn't even pay.

1296
00:49:55,544 --> 00:49:56,890
He's uses this
as a tooth pick!

1297
00:49:56,924 --> 00:49:59,824
That's for the gang that
couldn't shoot straight.

1298
00:49:59,858 --> 00:50:02,206
[laughs] A little prop humor.

1299
00:50:02,240 --> 00:50:04,484
Major. Look at this.

1300
00:50:09,040 --> 00:50:10,973
Nope. That's not
Glenn's, the whole

1301
00:50:11,008 --> 00:50:13,700
whirl is completely different.

1302
00:50:13,734 --> 00:50:15,115
This was Swanson's truck.

1303
00:50:15,150 --> 00:50:18,049
Gosh, you're good
my little detective.

1304
00:50:18,084 --> 00:50:20,258
[rumbling]

1305
00:50:20,293 --> 00:50:23,537
- What's that?
- I don't know.

1306
00:50:23,572 --> 00:50:25,229
[all scream]

1307
00:50:25,263 --> 00:50:27,714
Hey, that's what the
Mexican kid was sayin'.

1308
00:50:27,748 --> 00:50:30,544
[dark classical music]

1309
00:50:32,029 --> 00:50:33,789
You've looked better, Glenn.

1310
00:50:33,823 --> 00:50:36,136
"Oh, hi sis."

1311
00:50:36,171 --> 00:50:38,380
Get back, Joyce!

1312
00:50:40,244 --> 00:50:44,662
"Where was I? Now
where's my surprise toy?

1313
00:50:46,043 --> 00:50:48,217
"Ah, phooey.

1314
00:50:49,149 --> 00:50:51,082
"Ah, nothin.

1315
00:50:51,117 --> 00:50:55,328
"This is the last
time I high-jack and
organic food truck."

1316
00:50:57,157 --> 00:50:59,297
Sounds like Tim Allen.

1317
00:50:59,332 --> 00:51:02,369
[robots humming]

1318
00:51:05,545 --> 00:51:07,581
Oh no, I couldn't. I
couldn't eat another bite.

1319
00:51:07,616 --> 00:51:09,238
No, not bread.

1320
00:51:16,107 --> 00:51:17,315
I like to pick
out the raisins.

1321
00:51:17,350 --> 00:51:21,595
They are baking it the
way you want it, señor.

1322
00:51:21,630 --> 00:51:23,977
Put some tarragon
and mayo on it.

1323
00:51:24,011 --> 00:51:25,151
When will the rest be done?

1324
00:51:25,185 --> 00:51:26,600
They say it
would be ready soon.

1325
00:51:26,635 --> 00:51:28,533
They already loading the truck.

1326
00:51:28,568 --> 00:51:29,776
Good.

1327
00:51:29,810 --> 00:51:31,950
Want a cold cut with that?
A slice of cheese maybe?

1328
00:51:31,985 --> 00:51:33,538
Pickle?

1329
00:51:35,299 --> 00:51:36,886
Try it.

1330
00:51:36,921 --> 00:51:41,374
It really is delightful
bread. Mmm, flavor.

1331
00:51:41,408 --> 00:51:43,272
Can you taste anything?

1332
00:51:43,307 --> 00:51:46,172
Tastes like
bread. What's in it?

1333
00:51:46,206 --> 00:51:47,207
Chloral hydrate.

1334
00:51:47,242 --> 00:51:48,691
[all] [spitting with disgust]

1335
00:51:48,726 --> 00:51:51,867
It makes you go to
sleep for eight hours.

1336
00:51:53,075 --> 00:51:54,559
I'll be at the bakery.

1337
00:51:54,594 --> 00:51:57,562
With the butcher and
the candlestick maker.

1338
00:51:59,840 --> 00:52:02,533
We have to capture him and
this seems to be the best way.

1339
00:52:02,567 --> 00:52:03,844
Fill him up on bread?

1340
00:52:03,879 --> 00:52:05,639
Dr. Carmichael says it
can't harm him in the least.

1341
00:52:05,674 --> 00:52:08,918
How do you
handle a hungry man?

1342
00:52:08,953 --> 00:52:11,024
Isn't there any
other way to handle it?

1343
00:52:11,058 --> 00:52:12,198
No, we thought
about Saltines--

1344
00:52:12,232 --> 00:52:14,338
Let the Mexican
Authorities deal with him.

1345
00:52:14,372 --> 00:52:15,787
We have no other alternatives.

1346
00:52:15,822 --> 00:52:17,099
Nobody's taken the
time to think of any,

1347
00:52:17,134 --> 00:52:19,101
as far as I can see.

1348
00:52:19,136 --> 00:52:20,792
There isn't time to take.

1349
00:52:20,827 --> 00:52:22,725
If we don't get him before
he decides to move to another

1350
00:52:22,760 --> 00:52:25,590
mountain, we may lose
contact with him for weeks.

1351
00:52:25,625 --> 00:52:28,524
Yeah, Lord knows
he never writes.

1352
00:52:29,939 --> 00:52:31,907
I suppose you're right.

1353
00:52:31,941 --> 00:52:32,942
Yup.

1354
00:52:32,977 --> 00:52:37,533
His face. I can't
forget how horrible it was.

1355
00:52:37,568 --> 00:52:38,948
Where are you from, Joyce?

1356
00:52:38,983 --> 00:52:40,433
My home is in San Francisco.

1357
00:52:40,467 --> 00:52:41,882
But I live in Brittania.

1358
00:52:41,917 --> 00:52:43,056
Don't you think you'd
be better off back there?

1359
00:52:43,090 --> 00:52:44,230
Do you know what
it would be like

1360
00:52:44,264 --> 00:52:47,509
just sitting around
waiting for news?

1361
00:52:47,543 --> 00:52:48,958
You mind if I
suggest a remedy?

1362
00:52:48,993 --> 00:52:50,063
Try some bread.

1363
00:52:50,097 --> 00:52:51,202
It's an old one
but it usually works.

1364
00:52:51,237 --> 00:52:54,447
Find something else
to occupy your mind.

1365
00:52:54,481 --> 00:52:55,275
Sports, or somethin'.

1366
00:52:55,310 --> 00:52:56,552
Do you have a job?

1367
00:52:56,587 --> 00:52:58,830
I write copy for
an advertising agency.

1368
00:52:58,865 --> 00:53:01,039
Well, that should help
you forget your troubles.

1369
00:53:01,074 --> 00:53:02,524
I can imagine
myself going back

1370
00:53:02,558 --> 00:53:05,389
to writing all those
tired, old adjectives.

1371
00:53:05,423 --> 00:53:07,908
Tremendous, gigantic, colossal.

1372
00:53:07,943 --> 00:53:10,117
You know what'd they mean
to me now, don't you?

1373
00:53:10,152 --> 00:53:14,812
Glenn. A colossal freak major.

1374
00:53:14,846 --> 00:53:15,882
And he's my brother.

1375
00:53:15,916 --> 00:53:17,263
Look lady, we all
have our cross to bear.

1376
00:53:17,297 --> 00:53:20,369
We're all set major.

1377
00:53:22,854 --> 00:53:24,925
Time to make donuts.

1378
00:53:26,720 --> 00:53:29,965
[depressing classical music]

1379
00:53:29,999 --> 00:53:34,901
He ain't heavy. He's
my brother [crying].

1380
00:53:34,935 --> 00:53:36,558
Yep, that's
where we'll probably

1381
00:53:36,592 --> 00:53:38,284
put the butter over there.

1382
00:53:38,318 --> 00:53:40,182
Somethin' like that.

1383
00:53:43,150 --> 00:53:46,119
Secure the area where
you tracked Swanson's truck.

1384
00:53:46,153 --> 00:53:48,535
You ain't the boss of me.

1385
00:53:48,570 --> 00:53:50,157
Think he'll eat this stuff?

1386
00:53:50,192 --> 00:53:51,918
All depends on his appetite.

1387
00:53:51,952 --> 00:53:53,022
There hasn't
been a truck through

1388
00:53:53,057 --> 00:53:54,403
this road for two days now.

1389
00:53:54,438 --> 00:53:55,749
He should be hungry.

1390
00:53:55,784 --> 00:53:57,889
Let me drive. I
know this road best.

1391
00:53:57,924 --> 00:54:00,651
All right. There's
still no sign of him.

1392
00:54:00,685 --> 00:54:03,999
If we don't make contact,
we'll make the trip again.

1393
00:54:04,033 --> 00:54:05,587
A bread truck named Desire.

1394
00:54:05,621 --> 00:54:08,728
Tonight on Tennessee
Ernie Williams Theater.

1395
00:54:11,938 --> 00:54:14,734
Hey, let go of me!
What are you doing señor?

1396
00:54:14,768 --> 00:54:18,013
Hey, I've got a job to do!

1397
00:54:18,047 --> 00:54:22,397
Yoo-hoo, just drivin'’
a bread truck.

1398
00:54:22,431 --> 00:54:26,642
Fresh bread, mouthwatering
bread. Sliced thin.

1399
00:54:26,677 --> 00:54:29,507
Enjoy our bread
and keep the truck.

1400
00:54:29,542 --> 00:54:30,750
[sings] Somethin'
new is comin' to town,

1401
00:54:30,784 --> 00:54:32,614
and Jose the cop
is bringin' around.

1402
00:54:32,648 --> 00:54:35,893
[mysterious classical music]

1403
00:54:37,895 --> 00:54:38,896
Whoa.

1404
00:54:38,930 --> 00:54:40,138
Because we're
looking for a giant

1405
00:54:40,173 --> 00:54:42,865
that doesn't follow that
we don't value our lives.

1406
00:54:42,900 --> 00:54:45,868
Besides, we want
him to catch us.

1407
00:54:45,903 --> 00:54:48,526
At this speed,
he'll never make it.

1408
00:54:48,561 --> 00:54:50,735
I don't drive this way to
make you nervous, señores.

1409
00:54:50,770 --> 00:54:53,462
I'm driving this way 'cause
I ate some of that bread.

1410
00:54:53,497 --> 00:54:55,568
The further away [mumbles].

1411
00:54:55,602 --> 00:54:58,985
Besides, giants run very
fast. They have long legs.

1412
00:54:59,019 --> 00:55:03,092
Suddenly, you're the
expert on giants, huh?

1413
00:55:04,991 --> 00:55:07,442
The Bonneville Salt Flats,
where the world bread truck

1414
00:55:07,476 --> 00:55:11,377
land-speed record
trials are held.

1415
00:55:11,411 --> 00:55:12,999
Truckin' like
the Doo-Dah man.

1416
00:55:13,033 --> 00:55:15,035
He's right behind us.
You better step on it.

1417
00:55:15,070 --> 00:55:16,105
Before he does.

1418
00:55:16,140 --> 00:55:20,317
[suspenseful classical music]

1419
00:55:20,351 --> 00:55:22,767
Giant gentlemen prefer Hanes.

1420
00:55:22,802 --> 00:55:23,630
Nice gams.

1421
00:55:23,665 --> 00:55:25,770
You're getting close.

1422
00:55:25,805 --> 00:55:28,083
When we get to the
clearing, we'll slow down.

1423
00:55:28,117 --> 00:55:30,016
We better jump out.

1424
00:55:30,050 --> 00:55:31,293
If we make it.

1425
00:55:31,328 --> 00:55:32,950
We will.

1426
00:55:32,984 --> 00:55:35,401
Get ready.

1427
00:55:35,435 --> 00:55:37,748
Let's do this
again in 10 years.

1428
00:55:38,714 --> 00:55:43,098
Ready? Ready? Run!

1429
00:55:48,517 --> 00:55:51,175
Looks like an
episode of Cop Rock.

1430
00:55:51,209 --> 00:55:52,314
'Cause the rock and the...

1431
00:55:52,349 --> 00:55:53,453
[groans]

1432
00:55:53,488 --> 00:55:54,972
I know. I'm sorry

1433
00:55:56,629 --> 00:56:00,495
Roar. I smell something good.

1434
00:56:00,529 --> 00:56:01,910
Find a Chevy
truck, pick it up,

1435
00:56:01,944 --> 00:56:04,395
all day long you'll
have good luck.

1436
00:56:04,430 --> 00:56:07,433
Pretty truck.

1437
00:56:07,467 --> 00:56:10,919
[grunts]

1438
00:56:10,953 --> 00:56:14,888
What a pig. I mean he's
big and all but, really.

1439
00:56:14,923 --> 00:56:16,718
I think he likes
it. He's goin' for it.

1440
00:56:16,752 --> 00:56:20,066
He likes the rye. I told you
we shoulda made more rye.

1441
00:56:22,413 --> 00:56:26,313
What's your soup today?

1442
00:56:26,348 --> 00:56:28,971
Oh no! We smell like bread!

1443
00:56:29,006 --> 00:56:31,215
[all] Nooo!

1444
00:56:31,249 --> 00:56:33,907
Waiter, oh waiter?

1445
00:56:33,942 --> 00:56:37,601
Oh no, it backfired.
He's grown in 12 ways.

1446
00:56:37,635 --> 00:56:42,053
[suspensful classical music]

1447
00:56:42,088 --> 00:56:45,643
He ate too fast, it's
gone right to his head.

1448
00:56:45,678 --> 00:56:49,164
He's got a yeast-over.

1449
00:56:49,198 --> 00:56:50,855
KTLA predicts...

1450
00:56:50,890 --> 00:56:52,616
The case of Army
Colonel and Manning,

1451
00:56:52,650 --> 00:56:55,515
The Colossal Man who
went berserk in Las
Vegas not long ago,

1452
00:56:55,550 --> 00:56:57,690
- is in the news again today.
- Remember that?

1453
00:56:57,724 --> 00:56:58,898
He must have a good agent.

1454
00:56:58,932 --> 00:57:00,140
Military authorities
last night admitted,

1455
00:57:00,175 --> 00:57:01,866
that the earlier
announcement he had been

1456
00:57:01,901 --> 00:57:04,282
accidentally destroyed
was an error.

1457
00:57:04,317 --> 00:57:05,491
Hey look, nobody's perfect.

1458
00:57:05,525 --> 00:57:07,838
He was captured
alive today in Mexico.

1459
00:57:07,872 --> 00:57:09,287
And plans are underway
to fly him back

1460
00:57:09,322 --> 00:57:12,567
across the border in a
troop carrier transport.

1461
00:57:12,601 --> 00:57:14,776
Thing.

1462
00:57:14,810 --> 00:57:15,984
Tonight, the Kennedy Center

1463
00:57:16,018 --> 00:57:17,951
for the Arts presents Pavarotti.

1464
00:57:17,986 --> 00:57:20,022
You may quote me in
saying that the Nation

1465
00:57:20,057 --> 00:57:22,300
and its Representatives
and Congress have nothing

1466
00:57:22,335 --> 00:57:25,856
but gratitude for our Sister
Republic south of the border.

1467
00:57:25,890 --> 00:57:27,685
For their unstinting
cooperation.

1468
00:57:27,720 --> 00:57:28,721
And their bread.

1469
00:57:28,755 --> 00:57:30,101
And we're proud
of Major Baird,

1470
00:57:30,136 --> 00:57:32,932
and the way he handled the
problem of the Colossal Man.

1471
00:57:32,966 --> 00:57:34,865
It was a good job all around.

1472
00:57:34,899 --> 00:57:35,866
That's all for now.

1473
00:57:35,900 --> 00:57:36,970
Well, now that
he's being brought

1474
00:57:37,005 --> 00:57:38,558
back to the United States,

1475
00:57:38,593 --> 00:57:41,078
what does Congress
plan to do about him?

1476
00:57:41,112 --> 00:57:42,217
Do about whom?

1477
00:57:42,251 --> 00:57:43,218
I withdraw the question.

1478
00:57:43,252 --> 00:57:44,599
The giant man.

1479
00:57:44,633 --> 00:57:46,152
Oh, as far as I
know, that matter

1480
00:57:46,186 --> 00:57:48,395
doesn't come under
congressional jurisdiction.

1481
00:57:48,430 --> 00:57:51,778
I was given to understand the
Department of Medical Research

1482
00:57:51,813 --> 00:57:54,194
takes over from this point on.

1483
00:57:54,229 --> 00:57:55,472
Pass me that buck.

1484
00:57:55,506 --> 00:57:56,576
Fred Murtz?

1485
00:57:56,611 --> 00:57:59,441
More like bundts
and honeydew.

1486
00:57:59,476 --> 00:58:02,306
Naturally, we'll do what
we can in a scientific way.

1487
00:58:02,340 --> 00:58:04,273
But it's impossible
for this department

1488
00:58:04,308 --> 00:58:07,622
to assume the responsibility
of his feeding and custody.

1489
00:58:07,656 --> 00:58:09,037
So, we're putting
him up at the Watergate.

1490
00:58:09,071 --> 00:58:10,556
Why don't you take
this question up with

1491
00:58:10,590 --> 00:58:14,042
the Health and
Welfare Department?

1492
00:58:14,076 --> 00:58:15,561
Yes, that's my theme music.

1493
00:58:15,595 --> 00:58:16,734
The man in case was referred

1494
00:58:16,769 --> 00:58:18,874
to us here and
Health and Welfare,

1495
00:58:18,909 --> 00:58:20,186
but we found it laid outside the

1496
00:58:20,220 --> 00:58:22,602
scope of our original
appropriations.

1497
00:58:22,637 --> 00:58:24,052
So we turned him
out in the street.

1498
00:58:24,086 --> 00:58:25,156
I have an idea if he's
turned over to Congress

1499
00:58:25,191 --> 00:58:28,988
for action during
the present session.

1500
00:58:29,022 --> 00:58:31,577
I can't imagine who
told you to call me.

1501
00:58:31,611 --> 00:58:32,509
I told you not
to call me here.

1502
00:58:32,543 --> 00:58:33,682
The problem is it weren't for

1503
00:58:33,717 --> 00:58:35,097
the Legislative
Branch to settle.

1504
00:58:35,132 --> 00:58:37,652
Why don't you call the Pentagon?

1505
00:58:37,686 --> 00:58:41,448
Bert I. Gordon
sticks it to the man.

1506
00:58:41,483 --> 00:58:43,899
Guests of the Pentagon fly
U.S. Military Air Transport.

1507
00:58:43,934 --> 00:58:45,694
Recommended by John Sununu.

1508
00:58:45,729 --> 00:58:47,489
Tell the pilot to
circle the field once more.

1509
00:58:47,524 --> 00:58:48,766
The movie's not quite over.

1510
00:58:48,801 --> 00:58:51,528
U.S. 11034, you're to
circle the field again.

1511
00:58:51,562 --> 00:58:53,012
Roger.

1512
00:58:53,046 --> 00:58:54,600
He has enough fuel left
for five more minutes, Mayor.

1513
00:58:54,634 --> 00:58:55,877
Roger.

1514
00:58:55,911 --> 00:58:57,119
I can't understand you're
not giving your permission

1515
00:58:57,154 --> 00:58:58,431
for him to land under
those circumstances.

1516
00:58:58,465 --> 00:59:01,054
I think I've made our
position pretty clear.

1517
00:59:01,089 --> 00:59:03,022
We have no facilities
for a giant here.

1518
00:59:03,056 --> 00:59:04,402
But the sign said,
"Giants Welcome".

1519
00:59:04,437 --> 00:59:05,611
That plane runs out of gas,

1520
00:59:05,645 --> 00:59:06,991
it's coming down whether
you like it or not.

1521
00:59:07,026 --> 00:59:08,648
It can't stay any
longer, then it's necessary

1522
00:59:08,683 --> 00:59:10,650
to refuel and take off again.

1523
00:59:10,685 --> 00:59:11,824
Take off for where?

1524
00:59:11,858 --> 00:59:13,653
We need time to decide
what to do with him.

1525
00:59:13,688 --> 00:59:16,656
Can't you find us a warehouse
to keep him in temporarily?

1526
00:59:16,691 --> 00:59:19,625
Our warehouses are
all located in the
heart of the city.

1527
00:59:19,659 --> 00:59:20,764
Must be a nice downtown.

1528
00:59:20,798 --> 00:59:21,972
This creature's
presence there

1529
00:59:22,006 --> 00:59:23,560
would constitute too
great a police problem.

1530
00:59:23,594 --> 00:59:25,872
Now that applies to every
other large downtown building

1531
00:59:25,907 --> 00:59:28,323
including the Colosseum.

1532
00:59:28,357 --> 00:59:29,911
Have you considered
the Hollywood Bowl?

1533
00:59:29,945 --> 00:59:31,119
He's not ready for
the Hollywood Bowl.

1534
00:59:31,153 --> 00:59:32,983
But I can't have him
exposed to the weather.

1535
00:59:33,017 --> 00:59:34,260
He'’ll rust or something.

1536
00:59:34,294 --> 00:59:37,021
Well, I can't make
any further suggestions.

1537
00:59:37,056 --> 00:59:40,197
Well you're a big help.

1538
00:59:40,231 --> 00:59:41,025
Oh.

1539
00:59:41,992 --> 00:59:43,821
That hangar doesn't
seem to be in use.

1540
00:59:43,856 --> 00:59:45,892
Oh yes, my
coats--Oh that hangar.

1541
00:59:45,927 --> 00:59:47,204
That's impossible.

1542
00:59:47,238 --> 00:59:48,585
This airport is
one of the major

1543
00:59:48,619 --> 00:59:50,483
traffic centers of
the Western World.

1544
00:59:50,517 --> 00:59:51,553
That's why we're
so over-staffed.

1545
00:59:51,588 --> 00:59:52,865
Do you realize
what it would mean

1546
00:59:52,899 --> 00:59:54,383
if an uncontrollable monster
should get loose here?

1547
00:59:54,418 --> 00:59:55,799
I give you my word.

1548
00:59:55,833 --> 00:59:56,938
We won't keep him here a minute

1549
00:59:56,972 --> 01:00:00,113
longer than is
absolutely necessary.

1550
01:00:00,148 --> 01:00:01,080
OK.

1551
01:00:01,114 --> 01:00:02,150
All right.

1552
01:00:02,184 --> 01:00:04,704
Miss Hathaway, tell
them they can land.

1553
01:00:04,739 --> 01:00:06,292
Tell the ship to
come in for a landing.

1554
01:00:06,326 --> 01:00:08,087
Jethro, you can
come in for a landing.

1555
01:00:08,121 --> 01:00:12,401
U.S. 11034, you may
land on runway 1-7-0.

1556
01:00:15,646 --> 01:00:19,995
Hope Glenn took
some Dramamine.

1557
01:00:20,030 --> 01:00:20,893
That big guy was kickin' the

1558
01:00:20,927 --> 01:00:23,447
back of my seat the whole way.

1559
01:00:26,761 --> 01:00:29,971
Hey, look, the fly's open.

1560
01:00:31,869 --> 01:00:34,216
Look, he's levitating.

1561
01:00:34,251 --> 01:00:36,943
Glenn, turn over.

1562
01:00:36,978 --> 01:00:39,497
[grunting]
Sounds like a mini-bike.

1563
01:00:41,983 --> 01:00:43,985
Has anyone seen Glenn?

1564
01:00:44,019 --> 01:00:45,849
Why are you keeping
Glenn here at the airport?

1565
01:00:45,883 --> 01:00:47,057
Lady, do you
ever stop griping?

1566
01:00:47,091 --> 01:00:48,023
I'm holding him
here 'til Washington

1567
01:00:48,058 --> 01:00:49,438
decides what to do with him.

1568
01:00:49,473 --> 01:00:50,819
What to do with him?

1569
01:00:50,854 --> 01:00:52,821
He ought to be in a
hospital getting treatment.

1570
01:00:52,856 --> 01:00:54,720
They don't make
hospitals that big, Joyce.

1571
01:00:54,754 --> 01:00:55,928
Touché.

1572
01:00:55,962 --> 01:00:56,860
Besides, the
Doctors haven't turned

1573
01:00:56,894 --> 01:00:58,447
up anything that can help.

1574
01:00:58,482 --> 01:01:00,208
Why don't they treat
him with sulfhydryl?

1575
01:01:00,242 --> 01:01:02,072
Isn't that what they
said would cure him?

1576
01:01:02,106 --> 01:01:04,730
Well, they found it
stopped his growth but,

1577
01:01:04,764 --> 01:01:07,146
I'm afraid it won't reverse it.

1578
01:01:07,180 --> 01:01:08,492
I see.

1579
01:01:08,526 --> 01:01:11,115
He'll just have to get a
job and try to fit in somehow.

1580
01:01:11,150 --> 01:01:12,634
They can't keep
him here as if

1581
01:01:12,669 --> 01:01:14,187
he was some kind of cattle.

1582
01:01:14,222 --> 01:01:16,776
No, we're keeping him
here 'cause he's a big freak.

1583
01:01:16,811 --> 01:01:18,295
It's just for the time being.

1584
01:01:18,329 --> 01:01:19,986
I'm afraid the world
doesn't think of a

1585
01:01:20,021 --> 01:01:22,230
60 foot man the
way a sister does.

1586
01:01:22,264 --> 01:01:26,027
I read that on a
Hallmark card once.

1587
01:01:26,061 --> 01:01:29,582
Don't touch anything.

1588
01:01:29,616 --> 01:01:32,481
[grunting]

1589
01:01:35,968 --> 01:01:39,178
I'm taking down everything
he says, everything.

1590
01:01:39,212 --> 01:01:41,559
Sergeant Bilko,
what's he doin' here?

1591
01:01:43,078 --> 01:01:45,771
I said I hate futons.

1592
01:01:45,805 --> 01:01:48,877
I suppose it's necessary to
keep him tied down like this.

1593
01:01:48,912 --> 01:01:50,258
Yeah, it's fun too.

1594
01:01:50,292 --> 01:01:51,949
If I could trust him, I
could let him have more freedom.

1595
01:01:51,984 --> 01:01:54,365
As it is, I have
to play it safe.

1596
01:01:54,400 --> 01:01:57,368
This guy's inner child
is bigger than all of us.

1597
01:01:57,403 --> 01:01:58,922
Glenn Manning!

1598
01:01:58,956 --> 01:01:59,750
[screaming]

1599
01:01:59,785 --> 01:02:02,477
Yo, here! Right here.

1600
01:02:02,511 --> 01:02:05,618
As you see, he doesn't
even know his name.

1601
01:02:05,652 --> 01:02:08,966
Colonel Manning!
Colonel Manning!

1602
01:02:09,001 --> 01:02:10,796
You're just doin'
that to annoy him.

1603
01:02:10,830 --> 01:02:12,211
Colonel Manning!

1604
01:02:12,245 --> 01:02:15,248
[grunting]

1605
01:02:15,283 --> 01:02:19,494
What a dope. We are so
much better than he is.

1606
01:02:21,979 --> 01:02:23,981
I gotta scratch my
nose, for Pete's sake.

1607
01:02:24,016 --> 01:02:28,261
[new wave music]

1608
01:02:29,746 --> 01:02:31,713
[grunting]

1609
01:02:31,748 --> 01:02:34,026
Ready? Ah!

1610
01:02:34,060 --> 01:02:35,855
Missed. Try it again.

1611
01:02:35,890 --> 01:02:37,443
OK.

1612
01:02:37,477 --> 01:02:39,617
Harder, now all together!

1613
01:02:40,964 --> 01:02:43,000
Come on, like
we rehearsed it.

1614
01:02:43,035 --> 01:02:44,795
Like Chorus Line.

1615
01:02:44,830 --> 01:02:47,764
Tug-of-war, next is
the three-legged-race.

1616
01:02:47,798 --> 01:02:48,868
Pull it up.

1617
01:02:48,903 --> 01:02:51,940
It's like, really
hard sir. You try it.

1618
01:02:51,975 --> 01:02:55,081
[grunting loudly]

1619
01:02:58,015 --> 01:02:59,016
Who's tough?

1620
01:02:59,051 --> 01:03:01,398
All he has to do is let go.

1621
01:03:01,432 --> 01:03:05,367
Like the Battle
of the Network Stars
with Telly Savalas.

1622
01:03:05,402 --> 01:03:07,128
Love ya, baby.

1623
01:03:07,853 --> 01:03:09,164
Grrr.

1624
01:03:12,927 --> 01:03:16,033
Ow, rope burns, ow.

1625
01:03:16,931 --> 01:03:18,829
That cuts it,
we're outta here!

1626
01:03:18,864 --> 01:03:21,659
Got anymore bread?

1627
01:03:23,800 --> 01:03:27,907
[robot makes baby coos]

1628
01:03:27,942 --> 01:03:28,943
They're cute at that age.

1629
01:03:28,977 --> 01:03:30,876
Oh, he's walking now.

1630
01:03:30,910 --> 01:03:34,120
Yeah, looks like
the amazing white guy.

1631
01:03:34,155 --> 01:03:35,432
I'd go in and I'll shave.

1632
01:03:35,466 --> 01:03:38,607
We're gonna try
the guns now, sir.

1633
01:03:38,642 --> 01:03:39,885
Hold your fire.

1634
01:03:39,919 --> 01:03:41,645
Head him away from the
planes with tear gas.

1635
01:03:41,679 --> 01:03:43,198
Anesthetic would do
better than tear gas.

1636
01:03:43,233 --> 01:03:44,613
Get some here on the double.

1637
01:03:44,648 --> 01:03:46,167
Captain, get reinforcements.

1638
01:03:46,201 --> 01:03:47,168
Well, we got his sister.

1639
01:03:47,202 --> 01:03:48,307
What are they
gonna do to him?

1640
01:03:48,341 --> 01:03:49,584
I don't know,
but I can't let him

1641
01:03:49,618 --> 01:03:50,792
loose among a million people.

1642
01:03:50,827 --> 01:03:51,862
Are you gonna
have to kill him?

1643
01:03:51,897 --> 01:03:53,105
If it comes down to it I will.

1644
01:03:53,139 --> 01:03:54,554
No, no. Please.

1645
01:03:54,589 --> 01:03:56,211
I'm responsible for
the lives of others first.

1646
01:03:56,246 --> 01:04:00,043
I'm responsible for the
lives of over 400 people.

1647
01:04:00,077 --> 01:04:01,216
See that Miss Manning
gets back to her hotel.

1648
01:04:01,251 --> 01:04:03,322
Yes sir. Come on sis.

1649
01:04:03,356 --> 01:04:05,600
I know how it is, come on.

1650
01:04:09,431 --> 01:04:10,398
What's that?

1651
01:04:10,432 --> 01:04:13,470
Is that some
kind of new plane?

1652
01:04:13,504 --> 01:04:15,748
Why are there so many
civilians milling around here?

1653
01:04:15,782 --> 01:04:17,750
[people screaming]

1654
01:04:17,784 --> 01:04:21,512
Where's the blue
Concourse, lady?

1655
01:04:21,547 --> 01:04:23,618
[gun shots]

1656
01:04:23,652 --> 01:04:25,275
Hey.

1657
01:04:26,552 --> 01:04:29,762
I'm workin' a chick
here, get your own.

1658
01:04:30,970 --> 01:04:33,352
We seem to be
aggravating him, sir.

1659
01:04:39,082 --> 01:04:41,670
Somebody get a slingshot.

1660
01:04:41,705 --> 01:04:44,328
[gun shots]

1661
01:04:49,506 --> 01:04:53,165
Up, up and away?
I don't think so.

1662
01:04:54,925 --> 01:04:56,720
Oh, he's eatin'
a la carte there.

1663
01:04:56,754 --> 01:04:59,033
[laughs]

1664
01:05:00,103 --> 01:05:02,588
Stay together cheeks,
stay together cheeks.

1665
01:05:02,622 --> 01:05:03,830
It's an emergency.

1666
01:05:03,865 --> 01:05:06,592
Hello, Ripley's?
Believe it or not.

1667
01:05:06,626 --> 01:05:07,731
This is Major Baird here.

1668
01:05:07,765 --> 01:05:10,009
Get all the aircraft
into the air at once.

1669
01:05:10,044 --> 01:05:13,185
That goes for the
Air Force Jets.

1670
01:05:13,219 --> 01:05:15,118
Everything, even the
stuff that doesn't fly.

1671
01:05:15,152 --> 01:05:18,052
Even people, everybody,
hop in the air.

1672
01:05:18,086 --> 01:05:20,295
OK, we'll meet at the
sports bar after this.

1673
01:05:20,330 --> 01:05:21,503
Right. Kill the giant.

1674
01:05:21,538 --> 01:05:26,543
[plane engine whirs]

1675
01:05:28,027 --> 01:05:31,720
Stock footage away.

1676
01:05:31,755 --> 01:05:34,965
More stock footage away.

1677
01:05:35,000 --> 01:05:38,727
[grunting]

1678
01:05:38,762 --> 01:05:40,867
I think he's hungry again.

1679
01:05:42,214 --> 01:05:44,733
Oh good, Mr.
Drysdale is here.

1680
01:05:47,736 --> 01:05:50,325
Stay clear in case he falls.

1681
01:05:51,809 --> 01:05:53,121
[gun shot]

1682
01:05:53,156 --> 01:05:55,952
Win yourself a Kewpie
doll. Two shots for a dollar.

1683
01:05:55,986 --> 01:05:57,815
Win somethin' for
the little lady.

1684
01:05:57,850 --> 01:05:59,162
Ding.

1685
01:05:59,196 --> 01:06:00,577
It's a Salad Shooter.

1686
01:06:00,611 --> 01:06:04,098
You get a choice of vegetable
or juice. Here it is.

1687
01:06:04,132 --> 01:06:05,409
It's got kind
of a smoky taste.

1688
01:06:05,444 --> 01:06:07,101
That's a strong
taste definitely.

1689
01:06:07,135 --> 01:06:08,792
Look out, men!

1690
01:06:10,587 --> 01:06:14,591
I think he just
saw the check, sir.

1691
01:06:14,625 --> 01:06:16,110
Come on, I dare you
to go sit in the ice.

1692
01:06:16,144 --> 01:06:18,388
You got a big 'ol head.

1693
01:06:18,422 --> 01:06:20,528
A big 'ol head, yeah.

1694
01:06:21,598 --> 01:06:24,014
Hi Gretchen,
hi Paul, hi Keeba.

1695
01:06:24,049 --> 01:06:25,602
Now, that's over.

1696
01:06:25,636 --> 01:06:27,742
City council is up
in arms about this.

1697
01:06:27,776 --> 01:06:30,434
Public has a right to
protection, you know.

1698
01:06:30,469 --> 01:06:32,471
We understand your
position Mr. Mayor.

1699
01:06:32,505 --> 01:06:33,886
I came off of Washington for the

1700
01:06:33,920 --> 01:06:36,268
sole purpose of
straightening this thing out.

1701
01:06:36,302 --> 01:06:39,064
We're here to decide
what's to be done.

1702
01:06:39,098 --> 01:06:40,858
He's safe now, is he?

1703
01:06:40,893 --> 01:06:42,860
Well, he's too weak to
break loose at the moment.

1704
01:06:42,895 --> 01:06:44,103
Well, let's go play on him.

1705
01:06:44,138 --> 01:06:45,967
He's out of touch with
his wild beast inside.

1706
01:06:46,002 --> 01:06:48,866
He lost considerable blood
in his attempt to escape.

1707
01:06:48,901 --> 01:06:51,421
Besides that, we've taken
extra precautions with him.

1708
01:06:51,455 --> 01:06:52,284
Extra special precautions.

1709
01:06:52,318 --> 01:06:54,251
Let me show you.

1710
01:06:54,286 --> 01:06:57,496
OK, so when can
I take him home Doc?

1711
01:06:57,530 --> 01:06:59,636
We keep a watch on
him night and day.

1712
01:06:59,670 --> 01:07:01,017
Those manacles were
especially wrought

1713
01:07:01,051 --> 01:07:03,053
to stand 10 times
this man of strength.

1714
01:07:03,088 --> 01:07:04,089
These are the
chains he forged in...

1715
01:07:04,123 --> 01:07:05,297
We have them
anchored in cement

1716
01:07:05,331 --> 01:07:06,608
weighing two tons and
some 12 feet in the air.

1717
01:07:06,643 --> 01:07:07,920
[sniffing]

1718
01:07:07,954 --> 01:07:09,128
Guards have been doubled

1719
01:07:09,163 --> 01:07:10,992
and we keep a reserve
force in standby duty.

1720
01:07:11,027 --> 01:07:12,373
Smells like a
monkey house in here.

1721
01:07:12,407 --> 01:07:13,512
There's not much
chance he'll give us

1722
01:07:13,546 --> 01:07:15,341
anymore trouble
for the time being.

1723
01:07:15,376 --> 01:07:17,412
Well you certainly
have taken measures.

1724
01:07:17,447 --> 01:07:19,863
But you can't expect to
keep him here for life.

1725
01:07:19,897 --> 01:07:23,280
Have you any idea what'll
happen to him eventually?

1726
01:07:23,315 --> 01:07:24,868
Well, that depends.

1727
01:07:24,902 --> 01:07:26,042
On you, the viewer.

1728
01:07:26,076 --> 01:07:28,389
Do you see any hope
that he'll ever improve?

1729
01:07:28,423 --> 01:07:31,254
Well, I'd rather have
you ask Dr. Carmichael.

1730
01:07:31,288 --> 01:07:33,049
Dr. Carmichael.

1731
01:07:33,083 --> 01:07:35,465
Yes, this is Carmichael.

1732
01:07:35,499 --> 01:07:37,984
Dr. Carmichael, Mayor.

1733
01:07:38,019 --> 01:07:39,055
Can I call you Hoagie?

1734
01:07:39,089 --> 01:07:40,228
General Mills.

1735
01:07:40,263 --> 01:07:41,678
Major Nelson.

1736
01:07:41,712 --> 01:07:43,162
What can you tell 'em
about your patient, doctor?

1737
01:07:43,197 --> 01:07:44,543
Well he's tall for his age.

1738
01:07:44,577 --> 01:07:48,409
Well, the big
question now is his mind.

1739
01:07:48,443 --> 01:07:50,549
He may be suffering
from amnesia,

1740
01:07:50,583 --> 01:07:53,897
shell-shock, loss of memory,
whatever you want to call it.

1741
01:07:53,931 --> 01:07:55,071
In that case, we have techniques

1742
01:07:55,105 --> 01:07:56,796
now that'll bring him out of it.

1743
01:07:56,831 --> 01:08:01,560
On the other hand, if his brain
tissue has suffered injury,

1744
01:08:01,594 --> 01:08:04,666
he'll be a psychopathic case
and a menace until he dies.

1745
01:08:04,701 --> 01:08:06,116
Like Jerry Lewis?

1746
01:08:06,151 --> 01:08:08,429
Is there any way of telling?

1747
01:08:08,463 --> 01:08:09,947
We're starting him
on group on Monday.

1748
01:08:09,982 --> 01:08:10,983
An examination can do that.

1749
01:08:11,017 --> 01:08:12,778
How soon can you
proceed with it?

1750
01:08:12,812 --> 01:08:14,193
Almost immediately.
[phone rings]

1751
01:08:14,228 --> 01:08:15,194
After I take this call.

1752
01:08:15,229 --> 01:08:16,644
Please do so.

1753
01:08:16,678 --> 01:08:17,783
We'll decide what's to
be done with Manning

1754
01:08:17,817 --> 01:08:21,097
as soon as we have
the Doctor's findings.

1755
01:08:21,131 --> 01:08:22,098
We'll keep you posted, John.

1756
01:08:22,132 --> 01:08:23,271
[yawns loudly]

1757
01:08:23,306 --> 01:08:24,203
And I'll check
with you before

1758
01:08:24,238 --> 01:08:25,239
I return to Washington, Major.

1759
01:08:25,273 --> 01:08:26,412
Yes, sir.

1760
01:08:26,447 --> 01:08:28,690
Just end the scene
for cripes sake.

1761
01:08:28,725 --> 01:08:29,933
Drag it out.

1762
01:08:29,967 --> 01:08:32,211
Did I tell you that a man's
life depends on this plasma?

1763
01:08:32,246 --> 01:08:33,143
A big man.

1764
01:08:33,178 --> 01:08:34,662
I know 10 gallons is a lot,

1765
01:08:34,696 --> 01:08:37,009
but Dr. Carmichael explained
the case to you this morning.

1766
01:08:37,043 --> 01:08:38,321
And that's just for his hat.

1767
01:08:38,355 --> 01:08:39,391
I can't sit here
and argue about it,

1768
01:08:39,425 --> 01:08:42,463
it's needed right away.

1769
01:08:42,497 --> 01:08:43,774
Just a moment please.

1770
01:08:43,809 --> 01:08:45,328
Would you tell
him how big he is?

1771
01:08:45,362 --> 01:08:46,501
Dr. Carmichael.

1772
01:08:46,536 --> 01:08:48,296
Remember he's
big, keep lookin' up.

1773
01:08:50,436 --> 01:08:52,335
Will you talk to the
Red Cross people please?

1774
01:08:52,369 --> 01:08:54,440
They refuse to send
anymore plasma for Glenn.

1775
01:08:54,475 --> 01:08:56,166
They say we've used
too much already.

1776
01:08:56,201 --> 01:08:58,479
No, no. Tell
them I'm not here.

1777
01:08:58,513 --> 01:09:01,033
Tell them I paid the bill.

1778
01:09:01,067 --> 01:09:03,932
Help me out over here.

1779
01:09:03,967 --> 01:09:07,315
Seems like everyone is
afraid of the Red Cross.

1780
01:09:08,903 --> 01:09:11,112
Major Baird.

1781
01:09:11,147 --> 01:09:12,389
Annette and I will
see that you get

1782
01:09:12,424 --> 01:09:14,529
back all the blood we
used for this case.

1783
01:09:14,564 --> 01:09:16,669
Now, you'll send over
what we need immediately.

1784
01:09:16,704 --> 01:09:19,879
Type. Type O. Type
oh my God, he's huge.

1785
01:09:19,914 --> 01:09:21,536
Goodbye.

1786
01:09:22,503 --> 01:09:23,918
They'll have it
here in half an hour.

1787
01:09:23,952 --> 01:09:24,746
Or it's free.

1788
01:09:24,781 --> 01:09:28,888
Major, wait. I
want to thank you.

1789
01:09:28,923 --> 01:09:30,096
Forget it.

1790
01:09:30,131 --> 01:09:31,546
I'm sorry for what
I said yesterday.

1791
01:09:31,581 --> 01:09:33,997
I know you've tried to save
Glenn as much as you could.

1792
01:09:34,031 --> 01:09:35,550
You look tired, Joyce.

1793
01:09:35,585 --> 01:09:36,310
Sleep.

1794
01:09:36,344 --> 01:09:37,656
Isn't it time you went home?

1795
01:09:37,690 --> 01:09:39,520
I suppose it is.

1796
01:09:39,554 --> 01:09:40,762
There's an army
car waiting outside

1797
01:09:40,797 --> 01:09:42,868
if you would like
to go home in that.

1798
01:09:42,902 --> 01:09:45,905
With a soldier to
see me to the door?

1799
01:09:45,940 --> 01:09:47,355
This time, I'll do it myself.

1800
01:09:47,390 --> 01:09:51,187
That was an easy date.
General Smooth makes his move.

1801
01:09:51,221 --> 01:09:53,189
[snoring loudly]

1802
01:09:53,223 --> 01:09:55,570
Get your paws off my sister.

1803
01:09:55,605 --> 01:09:58,297
[suspenseful classical music]

1804
01:10:02,198 --> 01:10:04,614
Sure is big huh?
My little brother.

1805
01:10:04,648 --> 01:10:07,824
Can you believe it? How ironic.

1806
01:10:07,858 --> 01:10:11,621
"Had to leave, in the
circus end again. Ooh."

1807
01:10:17,178 --> 01:10:19,905
I just dreamed I was little.

1808
01:10:23,702 --> 01:10:26,843
Chains. My baby's got
me locked up in chains.

1809
01:10:26,877 --> 01:10:30,053
And they are the kind
that you can see.

1810
01:10:31,399 --> 01:10:33,815
Help, there's a normal
size person under my bed.

1811
01:10:33,850 --> 01:10:36,301
This is an
electro-encephalograph.

1812
01:10:36,335 --> 01:10:37,267
So don't touch it.

1813
01:10:37,302 --> 01:10:38,234
It records impulses set off

1814
01:10:38,268 --> 01:10:39,649
by different parts of the brain.

1815
01:10:39,683 --> 01:10:40,926
And it makes great taffy.

1816
01:10:40,960 --> 01:10:43,239
Records him on this paper,
making these wavy lines.

1817
01:10:43,273 --> 01:10:44,378
You hit this key,
you get a Samba.

1818
01:10:44,412 --> 01:10:45,620
We're gonna try to stimulate

1819
01:10:45,655 --> 01:10:48,554
your brother's mind
with various ideas.

1820
01:10:48,589 --> 01:10:50,901
If one happens to arouse
a response, it will cause

1821
01:10:50,936 --> 01:10:53,835
a tiny electric current
to occur in his brain

1822
01:10:53,870 --> 01:10:56,942
and that in turn will be greatly
amplified by this machine.

1823
01:10:56,976 --> 01:10:57,770
And greatly appreciated.

1824
01:10:57,805 --> 01:11:00,083
That will register
on this paper.

1825
01:11:00,117 --> 01:11:01,464
And what will that tell us?

1826
01:11:01,498 --> 01:11:02,810
Nothin', it's just for kicks.

1827
01:11:02,844 --> 01:11:07,021
If he responds to anything,
it means curable amnesia.

1828
01:11:07,055 --> 01:11:07,918
Or something, I forget.

1829
01:11:07,953 --> 01:11:09,989
If not...

1830
01:11:10,024 --> 01:11:10,818
I understand.

1831
01:11:10,852 --> 01:11:12,544
Good, will you
explain it to me?

1832
01:11:12,578 --> 01:11:13,510
I can't make heads
or tails out of it.

1833
01:11:13,545 --> 01:11:15,754
Will you get this out please?

1834
01:11:17,997 --> 01:11:21,173
Oh no. It's being repossessed
by the Military again.

1835
01:11:21,207 --> 01:11:22,347
[laughs]

1836
01:11:22,381 --> 01:11:23,900
Little Army joke.

1837
01:11:23,934 --> 01:11:27,628
Ah, to be young and be able
to push around a big machine.

1838
01:11:27,662 --> 01:11:31,839
OK. Surprise. Happy
Birthday to you...

1839
01:11:31,873 --> 01:11:35,774
Dr. Richardson, how's
the examination coming?

1840
01:11:35,808 --> 01:11:37,362
My nurse fell down [mumbles].

1841
01:11:37,396 --> 01:11:39,329
Getting along well,
I'll be down in a minute.

1842
01:11:39,364 --> 01:11:41,642
I'll get your
instrument ready.

1843
01:11:41,676 --> 01:11:43,229
Will you hook this up please?

1844
01:11:43,264 --> 01:11:45,335
Bill, bring the projector.

1845
01:11:45,370 --> 01:11:48,683
Get that cat
out of here. Meow.

1846
01:11:48,718 --> 01:11:50,823
Set it up over there.

1847
01:11:50,858 --> 01:11:52,584
Hey, check out
this blackhead,

1848
01:11:52,618 --> 01:11:55,380
it's as big as an anjou pear.

1849
01:11:55,414 --> 01:11:57,243
Screen going to be all
right outside, doctor?

1850
01:11:57,278 --> 01:11:59,936
His mouthwash
just ain't makin' it.

1851
01:11:59,970 --> 01:12:01,109
Yes, that's fine.

1852
01:12:01,144 --> 01:12:04,630
Glenn, this is a film about
V.D., watch it carefully.

1853
01:12:04,665 --> 01:12:08,945
Well, fellas, take your time
about setting the scene up.

1854
01:12:08,979 --> 01:12:10,912
Hey, hey, hey, we're
shootin' a movie here.

1855
01:12:10,947 --> 01:12:13,501
Hey, get off the set.

1856
01:12:13,536 --> 01:12:15,779
Oh, you belong there.

1857
01:12:18,920 --> 01:12:23,200
Will our first
contestant please come down?

1858
01:12:23,235 --> 01:12:25,271
We gotta go.

1859
01:12:25,306 --> 01:12:27,998
See ya. It's been
nice talkin' to ya.

1860
01:12:28,033 --> 01:12:31,519
See ya later, Glenn. Bye.

1861
01:12:31,554 --> 01:12:36,731
[doors closing hydraulically]

1862
01:12:41,357 --> 01:12:45,499
I am James Ungenteen Ktla,
and I predict your future.

1863
01:12:45,533 --> 01:12:46,638
These are the events
that will change

1864
01:12:46,672 --> 01:12:48,122
and illuminate your daily life.

1865
01:12:48,156 --> 01:12:50,193
Except, you aren't there yet.

1866
01:12:50,227 --> 01:12:51,919
Future date: 1998.

1867
01:12:51,953 --> 01:12:53,921
Ktla predicts personal
helicopters mean

1868
01:12:53,955 --> 01:12:55,923
freedom of travel for everyone.

1869
01:12:55,957 --> 01:12:57,442
Future date: 2105.

1870
01:12:57,476 --> 01:12:59,892
Ktla predicts richly
fulfilled future famulites

1871
01:12:59,927 --> 01:13:03,206
will enjoy a dinner of shrimp
paste underwater in aquadomes.

1872
01:13:03,240 --> 01:13:04,690
How? Why?

1873
01:13:04,725 --> 01:13:07,797
They'll travel to work and
play in personal two-man subs.

1874
01:13:07,831 --> 01:13:10,109
Thanks to the
Miracle Acrylic Dome.

1875
01:13:10,144 --> 01:13:12,111
All right. Let's work it now.

1876
01:13:12,146 --> 01:13:13,872
Future date: 2213.

1877
01:13:13,906 --> 01:13:16,461
Ktla predicts Alan Alda
revealed as anti-Christ,

1878
01:13:16,495 --> 01:13:17,669
and you are there.

1879
01:13:17,703 --> 01:13:19,049
Plus these extras:

1880
01:13:19,084 --> 01:13:20,568
Pope becomes grandmother.

1881
01:13:20,603 --> 01:13:24,054
Earl Scheib unable to
paint any car for 1995.

1882
01:13:24,089 --> 01:13:25,849
And the average
American will consume

1883
01:13:25,884 --> 01:13:27,782
15 times their weight in snow.

1884
01:13:27,817 --> 01:13:30,060
Wow. Take me to the bridge.

1885
01:13:30,095 --> 01:13:31,890
Future date: 2525.

1886
01:13:31,924 --> 01:13:35,445
Ktla predicts if man is still
alive, if women can survive,

1887
01:13:35,480 --> 01:13:39,000
they will find a stamp made
in the image of Martha Raye.

1888
01:13:39,035 --> 01:13:41,727
Cradle ports put you in
touch with your world.

1889
01:13:41,762 --> 01:13:43,522
Refreshing peppermint
soap proves

1890
01:13:43,557 --> 01:13:45,731
unsurpassed for 18 million uses.

1891
01:13:45,766 --> 01:13:47,768
All mild. All one.

1892
01:13:47,802 --> 01:13:50,943
Dilute for baby bath,
beach, body, shampoo,

1893
01:13:50,978 --> 01:13:53,256
sauna, and Chinese
essene burial ritual.

1894
01:13:53,290 --> 01:13:57,778
Dilute. Dilute. OK. All
One. Miracle of universe.

1895
01:13:57,812 --> 01:13:59,883
Impossible below PH3.

1896
01:13:59,918 --> 01:14:03,439
Absolute clean: Apply
Vaseline, oil, butter or cream.

1897
01:14:03,473 --> 01:14:05,579
Insert teaspoon
juicy lemon pulp.

1898
01:14:05,613 --> 01:14:07,822
Gotta go.

1899
01:14:07,857 --> 01:14:09,099
Has anybody seen him?

1900
01:14:09,134 --> 01:14:11,999
No. Tally Ho.

1901
01:14:12,033 --> 01:14:12,482
[barking]

1902
01:14:12,517 --> 01:14:16,762
[new wave music]

1903
01:14:19,800 --> 01:14:21,318
I was just actin'
screwy you guys.

1904
01:14:21,353 --> 01:14:22,630
I'll say.

1905
01:14:22,665 --> 01:14:23,631
I was just clownin' around.

1906
01:14:23,666 --> 01:14:25,909
Any opinion yet,
Dr. Richardson?

1907
01:14:25,944 --> 01:14:29,603
We're not ready to
publish yet. But he's big.

1908
01:14:29,637 --> 01:14:30,880
His parents
said he fell down.

1909
01:14:30,914 --> 01:14:32,364
He must have hit
his head on the rocks.

1910
01:14:32,398 --> 01:14:33,710
Of course the
wounds went untended,

1911
01:14:33,745 --> 01:14:37,852
so scar tissue
formed as you see it.

1912
01:14:37,887 --> 01:14:38,922
The shock of
such a fall would

1913
01:14:38,957 --> 01:14:40,648
indicate the
possibility of amnesia.

1914
01:14:40,683 --> 01:14:43,513
Milk of amnesia.

1915
01:14:43,548 --> 01:14:44,997
I don't understand it.

1916
01:14:45,032 --> 01:14:46,827
I get a normal,
nervous reaction,

1917
01:14:46,861 --> 01:14:49,588
that's certainly not
typical of amnesia.

1918
01:14:49,623 --> 01:14:51,694
Get a load of Mr.
Stage Business over there.

1919
01:14:51,728 --> 01:14:52,833
What's he doin'?

1920
01:14:52,867 --> 01:14:55,491
Are you ready for
the association tests?

1921
01:14:55,525 --> 01:14:56,664
You ready, Phil?

1922
01:14:56,699 --> 01:14:59,115
Oh, I'm in this
movie. I forgot.

1923
01:15:00,979 --> 01:15:04,569
Part one: toilet training.

1924
01:15:04,603 --> 01:15:09,919
Golden Books presents,
the World of Geography.

1925
01:15:09,953 --> 01:15:14,095
Glenn Manning. Look at
the picture in front of you.

1926
01:15:14,130 --> 01:15:15,338
I dare ya.

1927
01:15:15,372 --> 01:15:16,891
Does it mean anything to you?

1928
01:15:16,926 --> 01:15:18,859
It's your tax
dollars at work, Glenn.

1929
01:15:18,893 --> 01:15:20,999
You went to college
there, Manning.

1930
01:15:21,033 --> 01:15:23,104
[grunting]

1931
01:15:23,139 --> 01:15:25,935
We're gonna try another.
We're not getting any response.

1932
01:15:25,969 --> 01:15:27,626
Glenn, this is the Lusitania.

1933
01:15:27,661 --> 01:15:28,800
It has nothing to
do with your life,

1934
01:15:28,834 --> 01:15:30,595
but it was in the
packet in the gift shop.

1935
01:15:30,629 --> 01:15:32,286
Look again, Manning.

1936
01:15:32,320 --> 01:15:33,598
Look at this ship.

1937
01:15:33,632 --> 01:15:34,771
Your mother was on that ship.

1938
01:15:34,806 --> 01:15:35,841
It brought you back
to this country

1939
01:15:35,876 --> 01:15:37,256
after your service in Korea.

1940
01:15:37,291 --> 01:15:40,018
Your mother! Mother!

1941
01:15:41,398 --> 01:15:43,469
Does this bug you Glenn?

1942
01:15:43,504 --> 01:15:44,850
Try another one.

1943
01:15:44,885 --> 01:15:45,955
Glenn!

1944
01:15:45,989 --> 01:15:47,646
This is Sy Sperling,
President of Hair Club.

1945
01:15:47,681 --> 01:15:50,684
Look at this face.

1946
01:15:50,718 --> 01:15:51,926
Do you recognize it?

1947
01:15:51,961 --> 01:15:53,410
It's supposed to be you.

1948
01:15:53,445 --> 01:15:54,895
[grunting]

1949
01:15:54,929 --> 01:15:56,034
Won't spend much time on
this one, next slide please.

1950
01:15:56,068 --> 01:15:58,692
Does this man
mean anything to you?

1951
01:16:00,728 --> 01:16:01,591
You have five seconds.

1952
01:16:01,626 --> 01:16:03,800
Do you understand?

1953
01:16:03,835 --> 01:16:05,008
It's your face!

1954
01:16:05,043 --> 01:16:06,941
We'll come back to
this one if we have time,

1955
01:16:06,976 --> 01:16:08,840
but they're gonna
get harder now.

1956
01:16:08,874 --> 01:16:10,462
What do you think Doc?

1957
01:16:10,496 --> 01:16:11,636
I like the picture
of his college.

1958
01:16:11,670 --> 01:16:12,533
I don't see a
use in continuing.

1959
01:16:12,568 --> 01:16:14,328
He doesn't seem to respond.

1960
01:16:14,362 --> 01:16:16,330
Let me try, please.

1961
01:16:17,434 --> 01:16:18,677
Remember these.

1962
01:16:18,712 --> 01:16:21,887
You must
remember, Glenn, try.

1963
01:16:21,922 --> 01:16:25,822
Your childhood. Remember
your bicycle, Glenn?

1964
01:16:25,857 --> 01:16:27,997
It was red and it
had a light on it.

1965
01:16:28,031 --> 01:16:29,792
And you hit me with it.

1966
01:16:29,826 --> 01:16:31,897
We had fun when we were
kids, didn't we Glenn?

1967
01:16:31,932 --> 01:16:35,314
We had joy, we had fun, we
had seasons in the sun, Glenn.

1968
01:16:35,349 --> 01:16:37,420
Your first high school date.

1969
01:16:37,454 --> 01:16:41,355
When Dad let you take the
car. You were so proud.

1970
01:16:41,389 --> 01:16:42,839
Now you're an
enfeebled freak.

1971
01:16:42,874 --> 01:16:44,703
You must remember, Glenn.

1972
01:16:44,738 --> 01:16:45,980
You must remember this.

1973
01:16:46,015 --> 01:16:47,119
A kiss is still a kiss.

1974
01:16:47,154 --> 01:16:49,881
Please, Glenn.
Please try to remember.

1975
01:16:49,915 --> 01:16:52,987
[grunting loudly]

1976
01:16:54,610 --> 01:16:56,232
Glenn.

1977
01:16:56,991 --> 01:16:58,648
Oh my goodness, he's flashing

1978
01:16:58,683 --> 01:17:01,686
on the Uncle Dan in
the closet thing.

1979
01:17:01,720 --> 01:17:02,859
Glenn.

1980
01:17:02,894 --> 01:17:04,136
[screaming loudly]

1981
01:17:04,171 --> 01:17:08,002
You were a big help.
The slides were working.

1982
01:17:12,904 --> 01:17:17,356
I got nowhere else
to go. I got nothin'.

1983
01:17:17,391 --> 01:17:20,877
Giant fails last chance.
Giant fails last chance.

1984
01:17:20,912 --> 01:17:24,467
What does that mean?
He's gonna get the chair?

1985
01:17:24,501 --> 01:17:27,332
Now, how are they gonna
make a chair that big?

1986
01:17:27,366 --> 01:17:29,299
Have any money on him?

1987
01:17:30,507 --> 01:17:32,061
Get your orders?

1988
01:17:32,095 --> 01:17:34,926
Shipping him out
tomorrow morning.

1989
01:17:34,960 --> 01:17:37,929
The Navy has a ship ready
to take him to the island.

1990
01:17:37,963 --> 01:17:40,897
It's a small one about
60 miles off the coast.

1991
01:17:40,932 --> 01:17:43,313
Nice beach, good view.

1992
01:17:43,348 --> 01:17:45,212
It'll take all
night to get him ready.

1993
01:17:45,246 --> 01:17:48,146
Sponge bath alone will
take two, three hours.

1994
01:17:48,180 --> 01:17:49,216
Told Joyce yet?

1995
01:17:49,250 --> 01:17:50,976
No.

1996
01:17:51,011 --> 01:17:54,808
You better.
She's at her hotel.

1997
01:17:54,842 --> 01:17:57,880
I'll see you back here
in about a half an hour.

1998
01:17:57,914 --> 01:18:00,917
Thanks for the cheek marks
on the desk, Eisenhower.

1999
01:18:00,952 --> 01:18:04,093
[door closes]

2000
01:18:04,127 --> 01:18:06,854
Nah, I still don't get Zippy.

2001
01:18:08,960 --> 01:18:13,378
[suspenseful classical music]

2002
01:18:13,412 --> 01:18:16,588
Sounds kind of like Lurch.

2003
01:18:16,622 --> 01:18:17,900
He's all blocked up.

2004
01:18:17,934 --> 01:18:20,040
Think he's got an itch.

2005
01:18:20,074 --> 01:18:21,386
Love in the time of colitis.

2006
01:18:21,420 --> 01:18:22,421
He'll be well provided for.

2007
01:18:22,456 --> 01:18:23,871
An airlift is being
setup and food will

2008
01:18:23,906 --> 01:18:25,286
be parachuted down to him.

2009
01:18:25,321 --> 01:18:26,667
And he has a trust fund.

2010
01:18:26,702 --> 01:18:27,633
He'll be supplied
with everything he needs.

2011
01:18:27,668 --> 01:18:28,842
Is that a clock?

2012
01:18:28,876 --> 01:18:30,567
Just the same, it's horrible.

2013
01:18:30,602 --> 01:18:31,810
No, that's an astro-light.

2014
01:18:31,845 --> 01:18:32,742
There's no place
in the civilized

2015
01:18:32,777 --> 01:18:34,054
world for a creature that big.

2016
01:18:34,088 --> 01:18:35,193
So, we're sending
him to Cleveland.

2017
01:18:35,227 --> 01:18:36,504
Have you seen
what's happened to him?

2018
01:18:36,539 --> 01:18:37,816
He'll be happier by himself.

2019
01:18:37,851 --> 01:18:39,300
I haven't got time for this.

2020
01:18:39,335 --> 01:18:40,577
I suppose I should
agree with you,

2021
01:18:40,612 --> 01:18:44,996
but I can't help feeling
terribly sorry for him.

2022
01:18:45,030 --> 01:18:46,791
Well, look at it
this way, Glenn has

2023
01:18:46,825 --> 01:18:48,482
become a total stranger to you.

2024
01:18:48,516 --> 01:18:50,829
He's not your brother
anymore, but a monster.

2025
01:18:50,864 --> 01:18:52,900
With the instincts
of a wild beast.

2026
01:18:52,935 --> 01:18:54,799
But that happens
in families...

2027
01:18:54,833 --> 01:18:56,076
There's nothing you and
I, or anyone else can do

2028
01:18:56,110 --> 01:18:59,182
that will ever change him
back to what he once was.

2029
01:19:00,494 --> 01:19:02,254
Anchors away, Glenn [laughs].

2030
01:19:02,289 --> 01:19:03,946
I love teasing him like that.

2031
01:19:03,980 --> 01:19:05,948
Private, don't
tease the giant.

2032
01:19:05,982 --> 01:19:08,329
[suspenseful music]

2033
01:19:10,780 --> 01:19:15,233
Oh heck, stay together
cheeks. Excuse me.

2034
01:19:19,962 --> 01:19:23,966
[snoring jokingly]

2035
01:19:24,000 --> 01:19:26,900
It's kind of like the
great escape of Glenn's head.

2036
01:19:26,934 --> 01:19:30,075
[spooky classical music]

2037
01:19:41,017 --> 01:19:44,883
This is bull. I'm
outta here. This bites.

2038
01:19:44,918 --> 01:19:46,885
Will he be alone
on this island?

2039
01:19:46,920 --> 01:19:48,162
The Navy will
land an inspection.

2040
01:19:48,197 --> 01:19:49,129
What is this, 20 questions?

2041
01:19:49,163 --> 01:19:50,337
He's goin' to an
island. Come on.

2042
01:19:50,371 --> 01:19:51,925
I'd like to go
with him, if I can.

2043
01:19:51,959 --> 01:19:53,892
I thought you would. So
it's been arranged for you.

2044
01:19:53,927 --> 01:19:55,169
Goodnight Joyce.

2045
01:19:55,204 --> 01:19:55,791
Oh, wait.

2046
01:19:55,825 --> 01:19:56,999
Really?

2047
01:19:57,033 --> 01:19:57,931
I know you've done
everything you can

2048
01:19:57,965 --> 01:20:00,692
for him Mark, and for me.

2049
01:20:00,726 --> 01:20:01,797
Thank you.

2050
01:20:01,831 --> 01:20:03,453
Is that a big jack
with lights on it?

2051
01:20:03,488 --> 01:20:05,731
[phone rings]

2052
01:20:05,766 --> 01:20:07,181
It's just a lamp.

2053
01:20:07,216 --> 01:20:08,389
Hello?

2054
01:20:08,424 --> 01:20:10,529
Major Baird? Just a
moment, he's right here.

2055
01:20:10,564 --> 01:20:12,704
Hee-haw, it's Sam Wainwright.

2056
01:20:12,738 --> 01:20:14,706
Major Baird here.

2057
01:20:14,740 --> 01:20:16,604
[dramatic dark music]
When?

2058
01:20:16,639 --> 01:20:19,400
Well, he was checked
OK just 10 minutes ago.

2059
01:20:19,435 --> 01:20:20,712
Glenn has escaped.

2060
01:20:20,746 --> 01:20:22,162
He snuck out.

2061
01:20:22,196 --> 01:20:23,922
Let me speak
to Dr. Carmichael.

2062
01:20:23,957 --> 01:20:25,441
I can't sir, he's dead.

2063
01:20:25,475 --> 01:20:27,857
Therefore, unable
to come to the phone.

2064
01:20:27,892 --> 01:20:29,894
Round up the
usual large suspects.

2065
01:20:29,928 --> 01:20:31,516
This is an
all-points bulletin.

2066
01:20:31,550 --> 01:20:33,967
The Colossal Man is
loose in Los Angeles.

2067
01:20:34,001 --> 01:20:36,659
All units report
immediately upon contact.

2068
01:20:36,693 --> 01:20:40,490
I repeat: The Colossal Man
is loose in Los Angeles.

2069
01:20:40,525 --> 01:20:42,734
All units report
immediately upon contact.

2070
01:20:42,768 --> 01:20:44,322
Pep Boys and Dinettes.

2071
01:20:44,356 --> 01:20:45,633
[sirens wailing]

2072
01:20:45,668 --> 01:20:48,567
There's a giant on the loose
and he's got a big caboose.

2073
01:20:48,602 --> 01:20:50,915
Car 54, where are you?

2074
01:20:50,949 --> 01:20:53,607
Meanwhile, at
the Daily Planet...

2075
01:20:56,990 --> 01:20:58,025
Will they know where you are?

2076
01:20:58,060 --> 01:20:59,164
Hope not, wanna drink?

2077
01:20:59,199 --> 01:21:00,407
Yes, I told Harris we'll be

2078
01:21:00,441 --> 01:21:02,236
here in the office
waiting for news.

2079
01:21:02,271 --> 01:21:05,446
I've got a silk kimono,
mind if I slip into it?

2080
01:21:05,481 --> 01:21:07,034
Let me have the
police department.

2081
01:21:07,069 --> 01:21:08,864
I have't even
tried anything yet.

2082
01:21:08,898 --> 01:21:10,935
I'm calling for Major Baird.

2083
01:21:10,969 --> 01:21:12,212
Grrr.

2084
01:21:12,246 --> 01:21:14,421
Yes, we know he's escaped.
Has he been sighted yet?

2085
01:21:14,455 --> 01:21:16,561
Who is he, Mr.
Rogers all of a sudden?

2086
01:21:16,595 --> 01:21:17,907
I see.

2087
01:21:17,942 --> 01:21:19,978
Like the sporty
look? Is this OK?

2088
01:21:20,013 --> 01:21:23,913
No police reports,
he hasn't been seen.

2089
01:21:23,948 --> 01:21:25,846
Where could he be?

2090
01:21:25,881 --> 01:21:27,848
He signed with the
William Morris Agency,

2091
01:21:27,883 --> 01:21:30,333
now we'll never find him.

2092
01:21:31,956 --> 01:21:33,819
Quo Vadis, with
Peter Ustinov.

2093
01:21:33,854 --> 01:21:36,029
[phone rings]

2094
01:21:36,063 --> 01:21:38,859
Hello, shadow-y office? Grrr.

2095
01:21:38,894 --> 01:21:40,896
He's been spotted
in Griffith Park.

2096
01:21:40,930 --> 01:21:42,380
Do you have a map?

2097
01:21:42,414 --> 01:21:44,037
Yes, I've got the map of
Griffith Park right here.

2098
01:21:44,071 --> 01:21:46,936
I'm the same guy
in the same office.

2099
01:21:46,971 --> 01:21:48,179
Surround the
area and move the

2100
01:21:48,213 --> 01:21:49,628
infantry in on him
'til we make contact.

2101
01:21:49,663 --> 01:21:50,802
Yes sir.

2102
01:21:50,836 --> 01:21:52,424
Don't open fire 'til he's
isolated from all civilians.

2103
01:21:52,459 --> 01:21:53,529
Yes sir.

2104
01:21:53,563 --> 01:21:54,668
And then only if
absolutely necessary.

2105
01:21:54,702 --> 01:21:55,841
Yes sir.

2106
01:21:55,876 --> 01:21:57,464
Have the place block
off all access roads.

2107
01:21:57,498 --> 01:21:58,672
- Yes sir.
- They're good at that.

2108
01:21:58,706 --> 01:21:59,845
Move up our communications.

2109
01:21:59,880 --> 01:22:01,399
We'll setup the
headquarters in the hills.

2110
01:22:01,433 --> 01:22:02,089
Yes sir.

2111
01:22:02,124 --> 01:22:03,849
I'll take command.

2112
01:22:03,884 --> 01:22:06,507
Right.

2113
01:22:07,129 --> 01:22:08,854
I declare Martial law.

2114
01:22:08,889 --> 01:22:11,547
Attention all units.
Attention all units.

2115
01:22:11,581 --> 01:22:14,377
The Colossal Man is in
the Griffith Park area.

2116
01:22:14,412 --> 01:22:18,657
Get the description right.
Male, Caucasian. 60 feet tall.

2117
01:22:18,692 --> 01:22:22,938
Answers to the name
Glenn or Hey Big Guy.

2118
01:22:22,972 --> 01:22:25,009
Proceed to the
Ferndale entrance.

2119
01:22:25,043 --> 01:22:28,288
Road blocks will
be setup at once.

2120
01:22:28,322 --> 01:22:29,427
We interrupt our
regular program to

2121
01:22:29,461 --> 01:22:31,463
bring you a special
police bulletin.

2122
01:22:31,498 --> 01:22:33,810
The Colossal Man
has broken loose.

2123
01:22:33,845 --> 01:22:37,228
And is now known to be in the
vicinity of Griffith Park.

2124
01:22:37,262 --> 01:22:38,919
Steps are being taken
to recapture him.

2125
01:22:38,954 --> 01:22:40,093
It's Jack Lemmon.

2126
01:22:40,127 --> 01:22:42,095
Do not go near
the area. I repeat.

2127
01:22:42,129 --> 01:22:44,649
Do not go near the area.

2128
01:22:44,683 --> 01:22:45,891
You may endanger your
life by doing so.

2129
01:22:45,926 --> 01:22:46,962
Hey, let's go near the area.

2130
01:22:46,996 --> 01:22:48,273
You are certain
to impede emergency

2131
01:22:48,308 --> 01:22:51,380
services which need all
the space they can get.

2132
01:22:51,414 --> 01:22:53,382
This station is dispatching
a mobile unit for

2133
01:22:53,416 --> 01:22:56,419
on the spot coverage at
the scene in Griffith Park.

2134
01:22:56,454 --> 01:22:59,491
Ktla, over and out.
Keep watching the skies.

2135
01:22:59,526 --> 01:23:00,837
Oh, I see.

2136
01:23:00,872 --> 01:23:04,531
They let the bus from Duluth
go near the area, but we can't.

2137
01:23:04,565 --> 01:23:07,706
It's the cast of
Rebel Without a Cause.

2138
01:23:07,741 --> 01:23:09,087
They get to go near the area.

2139
01:23:09,122 --> 01:23:11,607
[people chattering]

2140
01:23:13,022 --> 01:23:16,922
Car 54, where are you?

2141
01:23:16,957 --> 01:23:18,683
One Adam 12. See the man.

2142
01:23:18,717 --> 01:23:21,548
Neighbors report giant man
kicking over garbage cans.

2143
01:23:21,582 --> 01:23:23,239
Hey bud, pull out.

2144
01:23:23,274 --> 01:23:24,689
I'm waiting for
a load of kids.

2145
01:23:24,723 --> 01:23:25,448
Where are they?

2146
01:23:25,483 --> 01:23:26,932
In the Griffith Observatory,

2147
01:23:26,967 --> 01:23:28,175
lookin' for a
sputnik or something.

2148
01:23:28,210 --> 01:23:29,245
Hurry them up and get out of

2149
01:23:29,280 --> 01:23:30,557
the park as quick as you can.

2150
01:23:30,591 --> 01:23:31,696
What's gonna happen?

2151
01:23:31,730 --> 01:23:34,147
There's trouble. The giant
man is loose around here.

2152
01:23:34,181 --> 01:23:35,665
What, are these
kids in night-school?

2153
01:23:35,700 --> 01:23:39,083
No. Day for night-school.
It's Hollywood.

2154
01:23:39,117 --> 01:23:41,119
Let's go. Hurry
it up. All aboard.

2155
01:23:41,154 --> 01:23:43,052
Come on, I got a bus to move.

2156
01:23:43,087 --> 01:23:45,365
Ha. Bust-a-move.

2157
01:23:47,194 --> 01:23:51,716
[new wave music]

2158
01:23:52,786 --> 01:23:55,064
Can I have a gun? You
said I could have a gun.

2159
01:23:55,099 --> 01:23:56,859
Setup a road block here.

2160
01:23:56,893 --> 01:23:58,826
He's somewhere between
us and the observatory.

2161
01:23:58,861 --> 01:23:59,724
A road block?

2162
01:23:59,758 --> 01:24:00,932
Why don't you just draw a line

2163
01:24:00,966 --> 01:24:03,314
on the highway and tell
him not to cross it?

2164
01:24:03,348 --> 01:24:04,660
Give me my boom box.

2165
01:24:04,694 --> 01:24:07,214
[suspenseful music]

2166
01:24:09,837 --> 01:24:13,151
Yep. A lot of films
crashed on this curve.

2167
01:24:18,708 --> 01:24:21,021
Check it out. It's
the Tonight Show's set.

2168
01:24:21,056 --> 01:24:22,747
I didn't know that.

2169
01:24:22,781 --> 01:24:23,955
So that's what they're doin'.

2170
01:24:23,989 --> 01:24:26,371
Jeez, we're really
up here aren't we?

2171
01:24:30,099 --> 01:24:31,445
We're gonna go with
our Haggar slacks now?

2172
01:24:31,480 --> 01:24:32,481
Hmm-mmm.

2173
01:24:32,515 --> 01:24:33,792
Nice.

2174
01:24:33,827 --> 01:24:36,726
This is Major Baird at
the Griffith Park road block.

2175
01:24:36,761 --> 01:24:38,797
You're not gonna believe
where I'm calling you from.

2176
01:24:38,832 --> 01:24:41,421
I'm outside, on a shoe phone.

2177
01:24:43,078 --> 01:24:45,045
Oh, it's a premier.

2178
01:24:45,080 --> 01:24:49,118
They're gonna send
out Army Archer next.

2179
01:24:49,153 --> 01:24:52,190
It's a used car
sell-a-thon. What a feeling.

2180
01:24:55,642 --> 01:24:57,885
Ladies and gentlemen,
you're witnessing a manhunt--

2181
01:24:57,920 --> 01:24:59,094
Ktla is everywhere.

2182
01:24:59,128 --> 01:25:01,406
For the biggest
man in existence.

2183
01:25:01,441 --> 01:25:06,066
We're in Griffith Park. It's
been surrounded by troops.

2184
01:25:06,101 --> 01:25:07,309
Big troops.

2185
01:25:07,343 --> 01:25:11,071
They're moving out
to try to make contact.

2186
01:25:11,106 --> 01:25:12,969
We're hoping he breaks into
song when we lights at him.

2187
01:25:13,004 --> 01:25:13,936
That building
your seeing there

2188
01:25:13,970 --> 01:25:16,559
is the Griffith Observatory.

2189
01:25:16,594 --> 01:25:20,529
It's a nice place. You
should visit sometime.

2190
01:25:20,563 --> 01:25:23,325
The giant is tearing
people apart like bread.

2191
01:25:23,359 --> 01:25:26,569
No, I just added that.
Journalistic license, and all.

2192
01:25:26,604 --> 01:25:28,985
Anyway, back to Rebel
Without a Cause.

2193
01:25:29,020 --> 01:25:31,022
Remember, this is brought to you

2194
01:25:31,056 --> 01:25:34,025
by True Value Hardware stores.

2195
01:25:34,059 --> 01:25:36,821
Put your clothes
on, it's the cops.

2196
01:25:37,960 --> 01:25:39,858
I want a jury to cover
the mountain on all sides.

2197
01:25:39,893 --> 01:25:41,066
A jury? What kinda jury?

2198
01:25:41,101 --> 01:25:43,207
Particularly here and here.

2199
01:25:46,348 --> 01:25:48,902
Funny-face tank.

2200
01:25:48,936 --> 01:25:50,800
Cuddly funny tank.

2201
01:25:51,870 --> 01:25:54,390
This is the tanks I get.

2202
01:25:55,080 --> 01:25:57,876
That was a real Jeep joke.

2203
01:25:57,911 --> 01:26:01,501
Get the Bat Signal ready.

2204
01:26:01,535 --> 01:26:03,537
Flashlight tag. You're it.

2205
01:26:04,814 --> 01:26:09,716
Wait, I'm not ready. Two,
three, four. How does it start?

2206
01:26:09,750 --> 01:26:11,027
He's parked on an
incline, you're gonna

2207
01:26:11,062 --> 01:26:14,272
want to turn those
wheels probably.

2208
01:26:14,307 --> 01:26:17,896
Rat patrol, pass it on.
Rat patrol, pass it on.

2209
01:26:20,451 --> 01:26:24,696
Arthur? Laurie?
Hurry, we're leaving now.

2210
01:26:24,731 --> 01:26:27,768
Sure, I'd go up in a rocket
ship. What's to stop me?

2211
01:26:27,803 --> 01:26:29,529
They've got all of
the problems solved.

2212
01:26:29,563 --> 01:26:31,393
You wouldn't have
anybody to talk to.

2213
01:26:31,427 --> 01:26:32,877
This kid has a rocket
in his pocket I think.

2214
01:26:32,911 --> 01:26:34,948
I'm cold. I wish Mom
would've made me bring my coat.

2215
01:26:34,982 --> 01:26:36,260
Girls mature faster.

2216
01:26:36,294 --> 01:26:37,882
I'd talk to all the people
back on earth by radio.

2217
01:26:37,916 --> 01:26:39,953
Maybe I'd even call you, Laurie.

2218
01:26:39,987 --> 01:26:40,988
Laurie.

2219
01:26:41,023 --> 01:26:42,093
That way, when your
father's objecting to you

2220
01:26:42,127 --> 01:26:43,612
talking on the phone too long,

2221
01:26:43,646 --> 01:26:45,614
you just tell him it
was someone on the moon.

2222
01:26:45,648 --> 01:26:47,443
That wouldn't make any
difference to my father.

2223
01:26:47,478 --> 01:26:49,100
What, are we suddenly
in a different movie?

2224
01:26:49,134 --> 01:26:50,998
What's going on?

2225
01:26:51,033 --> 01:26:52,724
Maybe the
martians have landed.

2226
01:26:52,759 --> 01:26:54,692
The teacher's
been calling us.

2227
01:26:54,726 --> 01:26:55,762
Let her call.

2228
01:26:55,796 --> 01:26:58,074
Boy, look at all
those search lights.

2229
01:26:58,109 --> 01:26:59,352
Ktla knows you.

2230
01:26:59,386 --> 01:27:01,733
We're going to meet
some of the officers.

2231
01:27:01,768 --> 01:27:03,459
We couldn't have
done it without him.

2232
01:27:03,494 --> 01:27:04,840
Any trouble with the public

2233
01:27:04,874 --> 01:27:07,360
causing congestion
in the disaster area?

2234
01:27:07,394 --> 01:27:08,533
Not this time.

2235
01:27:08,568 --> 01:27:09,500
They seem to be
keeping the roads

2236
01:27:09,534 --> 01:27:10,604
pretty well open for us.

2237
01:27:10,639 --> 01:27:11,812
Sounds like the
cop's kinda congested.

2238
01:27:11,847 --> 01:27:13,366
I understand you were
able to get everyone out

2239
01:27:13,400 --> 01:27:14,021
of the park before anything
could happen to them.

2240
01:27:14,056 --> 01:27:15,540
That's right.

2241
01:27:15,575 --> 01:27:17,508
We really appreciate you
men at a time like this.

2242
01:27:17,542 --> 01:27:19,579
Quit kissing up, Ktla.

2243
01:27:19,613 --> 01:27:21,926
Oh, there's Glenn now. That's
the top of the observatory.

2244
01:27:21,960 --> 01:27:25,378
[people murmuring]

2245
01:27:25,412 --> 01:27:27,863
Oh officer, there was
a tour bus from Westmont

2246
01:27:27,897 --> 01:27:30,072
Junior High up at the
planetarium tonight.

2247
01:27:30,106 --> 01:27:31,522
Did you see it come down?

2248
01:27:31,556 --> 01:27:33,972
All cars were checked out
of the park sometime ago.

2249
01:27:34,007 --> 01:27:35,008
Are you sure?

2250
01:27:35,042 --> 01:27:36,389
My daughter Laurie was on it.

2251
01:27:36,423 --> 01:27:38,460
I've been waiting for
her down at the corner.

2252
01:27:38,494 --> 01:27:39,875
You see, we live in Glendale.

2253
01:27:39,909 --> 01:27:41,601
And I thought if I
picked her up here

2254
01:27:41,635 --> 01:27:42,774
and drove her home, she
could get her sleep.

2255
01:27:42,809 --> 01:27:44,017
Yeah, yeah. Make
it short, sister.

2256
01:27:44,051 --> 01:27:45,260
I got things to do.

2257
01:27:45,294 --> 01:27:46,433
She has to get up so
early on school days.

2258
01:27:46,468 --> 01:27:48,608
I brought her coat
in case she's cold.

2259
01:27:48,642 --> 01:27:49,885
Wait a minute and I'll check.

2260
01:27:49,919 --> 01:27:51,749
Thank you.

2261
01:27:51,783 --> 01:27:54,165
- Arthur? Laurie?
- We better go.

2262
01:27:54,199 --> 01:27:56,995
Cut it out mom,
I'm makin' time.

2263
01:27:58,756 --> 01:28:00,482
There's nothing for
you to worry about.

2264
01:28:00,516 --> 01:28:02,794
The bus is still up there
but it seems to be safe.

2265
01:28:02,829 --> 01:28:05,487
Can't I go through? Let
me go to Laurie, please.

2266
01:28:05,521 --> 01:28:06,729
She ought to have her coat.

2267
01:28:06,764 --> 01:28:08,628
I wanted her to take it
when she left the house.

2268
01:28:08,662 --> 01:28:11,113
Yeah, you told
me that already.

2269
01:28:11,147 --> 01:28:12,804
She'll be all
right without it.

2270
01:28:12,839 --> 01:28:14,703
Just you take it easy
and wait right here.

2271
01:28:14,737 --> 01:28:16,843
Lost in the valley of
the shadow of the sub-plot.

2272
01:28:16,877 --> 01:28:19,121
Laurie Edwardson, Arthur
Lang, where have you been?

2273
01:28:19,155 --> 01:28:20,260
Coming of age?

2274
01:28:20,295 --> 01:28:21,779
Over there,
watchin' the lights.

2275
01:28:21,813 --> 01:28:23,332
They're a lot of
people down there.

2276
01:28:23,367 --> 01:28:24,609
Something's the matter.

2277
01:28:24,644 --> 01:28:26,266
Well get in the bus,
quickly. We have to go.

2278
01:28:26,301 --> 01:28:28,544
Look, look at the giant!

2279
01:28:28,579 --> 01:28:30,788
Oh yeah, hi. How are ya?
I get that all the time.

2280
01:28:30,822 --> 01:28:33,963
Seen it. I'm a
lineman for the county.

2281
01:28:36,276 --> 01:28:37,933
Ktla, no.

2282
01:28:37,967 --> 01:28:39,037
He's tremendous.

2283
01:28:39,072 --> 01:28:40,694
[people exclaiming]

2284
01:28:40,729 --> 01:28:43,973
Wait, I think I see Humphrey
Bogart in Emot Ett Kvitto.

2285
01:28:44,008 --> 01:28:48,530
[mysterious music]

2286
01:28:52,534 --> 01:28:54,398
A Spielberg film
all of a sudden.

2287
01:28:54,432 --> 01:28:56,814
Ladies and gentlemen,
thank you for being here.

2288
01:28:56,848 --> 01:28:58,540
I didn't know it was
gonna be stand up only.

2289
01:28:58,574 --> 01:29:00,956
I used to live in a circus tent.

2290
01:29:02,820 --> 01:29:05,512
Glenn Manning.
You are surrounded.

2291
01:29:05,547 --> 01:29:06,686
By people who love you.

2292
01:29:06,720 --> 01:29:08,343
It is impossible
for you to escape.

2293
01:29:08,377 --> 01:29:10,793
We wanna show
you more slides.

2294
01:29:12,001 --> 01:29:14,314
[grunting]

2295
01:29:14,349 --> 01:29:15,350
Is that Ktla down there?

2296
01:29:15,384 --> 01:29:16,903
I wanna talk straight to Ktla.

2297
01:29:16,937 --> 01:29:19,077
[grunting]

2298
01:29:19,112 --> 01:29:22,805
I repeat. Escape
is impossible.

2299
01:29:22,840 --> 01:29:26,015
You won't be harmed if you
give yourself up at once.

2300
01:29:26,050 --> 01:29:28,673
I've heard that before.

2301
01:29:30,434 --> 01:29:31,814
Give yourself up, Glenn.

2302
01:29:31,849 --> 01:29:32,919
To a higher power.

2303
01:29:32,953 --> 01:29:34,921
Give yourself up.

2304
01:29:34,955 --> 01:29:37,337
Surrender yourself or
you will be destroyed.

2305
01:29:37,372 --> 01:29:39,132
I'll see they go easy on ya.

2306
01:29:39,166 --> 01:29:41,686
[ominous music]

2307
01:29:43,826 --> 01:29:45,621
[grunting]

2308
01:29:45,656 --> 01:29:49,004
Come on cop-per, you'll
never take me alive.

2309
01:29:50,246 --> 01:29:52,732
Grr. Grr.

2310
01:29:52,766 --> 01:29:56,011
Spotlight dance,
Glenn Manning.

2311
01:29:59,186 --> 01:30:01,982
[screams loudly]

2312
01:30:02,017 --> 01:30:02,811
[children screaming]

2313
01:30:02,845 --> 01:30:04,260
Oh Willy, I
can see the house.

2314
01:30:04,295 --> 01:30:08,195
That's Laurie's bus. Please
let me go. That's Laurie.

2315
01:30:08,230 --> 01:30:11,406
Don't you think somebody's
got to do something?

2316
01:30:11,440 --> 01:30:13,753
I've seen bigger
guys than that before.

2317
01:30:15,099 --> 01:30:17,998
This is a job for Superman.

2318
01:30:18,033 --> 01:30:20,104
[sobbing]

2319
01:30:21,381 --> 01:30:24,177
[mumbling]
Hey, stop, wait, come back.

2320
01:30:24,211 --> 01:30:25,868
Joyce, come back.

2321
01:30:25,903 --> 01:30:27,939
You still have to
write Fennegan's Wake.

2322
01:30:27,974 --> 01:30:31,046
[ominous music]

2323
01:30:31,080 --> 01:30:32,530
Hey, hold the bus.

2324
01:30:32,565 --> 01:30:35,499
[children screaming]

2325
01:30:44,646 --> 01:30:47,165
- Laurie!
- Stella!

2326
01:30:47,200 --> 01:30:49,098
McCloud!

2327
01:30:49,133 --> 01:30:52,101
[crying] Laurie.

2328
01:30:57,866 --> 01:31:01,386
Glenn W. Manning, you come
right down here this instant.

2329
01:31:01,421 --> 01:31:03,112
Put that down right now.

2330
01:31:03,147 --> 01:31:04,389
Put it down.

2331
01:31:04,424 --> 01:31:06,461
I'm the God, I'm the God!

2332
01:31:06,495 --> 01:31:08,255
Listen to me Glenn, please.

2333
01:31:08,290 --> 01:31:09,222
I'd call this a good argument

2334
01:31:09,256 --> 01:31:10,672
against bussing, wouldn't you?

2335
01:31:10,706 --> 01:31:11,604
What if you bus
your kids, they

2336
01:31:11,638 --> 01:31:13,398
might get picked up by a giant?

2337
01:31:13,433 --> 01:31:14,296
You never know.

2338
01:31:14,330 --> 01:31:15,470
Put them down.

2339
01:31:15,504 --> 01:31:17,437
[children screaming]

2340
01:31:17,472 --> 01:31:19,957
Put them down, Glenn.

2341
01:31:21,027 --> 01:31:23,512
This is really getting
embarrassing, Glenn.

2342
01:31:23,547 --> 01:31:25,825
Put them down.

2343
01:31:26,619 --> 01:31:30,001
Put them down Glenn, please.

2344
01:31:30,036 --> 01:31:31,140
"I'll put 'em down."

2345
01:31:32,279 --> 01:31:37,146
And don't tilt it
either. On the tires!

2346
01:31:37,181 --> 01:31:39,217
Ow. ow.

2347
01:31:41,047 --> 01:31:42,980
OK, now pick it up again.
Come on, 10 more reps.

2348
01:31:43,014 --> 01:31:47,812
Please Glenn,
listen. Try to think.

2349
01:31:47,847 --> 01:31:50,574
You must, Glenn
please. Try to think.

2350
01:31:50,608 --> 01:31:53,197
You try to think
but nothin' happens.

2351
01:31:53,231 --> 01:31:56,787
Oh my God, I'm
Sinead O'Connor.

2352
01:31:56,821 --> 01:32:00,031
Wait here with me, Glenn.

2353
01:32:00,066 --> 01:32:01,446
They're coming for us.

2354
01:32:01,481 --> 01:32:02,758
They're coming to
take us away, ha-ha.

2355
01:32:02,793 --> 01:32:05,796
They're coming to take us
away, ho-ho, he-he, ha-ha.

2356
01:32:05,830 --> 01:32:09,593
Sis, pull my finger.

2357
01:32:09,627 --> 01:32:12,837
What am I? What am I?
Get it? Sistine Chapel.

2358
01:32:12,872 --> 01:32:17,462
Like that? I got another
one too. Watch the bunny.

2359
01:32:17,497 --> 01:32:19,326
Joyce.

2360
01:32:19,361 --> 01:32:21,915
So Glenn is pro-Joyce.

2361
01:32:25,332 --> 01:32:28,888
Aw, forget it.
Nobody loves me.

2362
01:32:28,922 --> 01:32:30,855
I gotta get some
air. It's a big country.

2363
01:32:30,890 --> 01:32:34,376
I better go see part
of it before I die.

2364
01:32:34,410 --> 01:32:37,034
Wait, Glenn, wait.

2365
01:32:38,898 --> 01:32:41,245
If you walk away now,
there's gonna be another sequel.

2366
01:32:41,279 --> 01:32:43,972
Oh, no.

2367
01:32:44,006 --> 01:32:47,492
Glenn? Glenn,
what are you doing?

2368
01:32:47,527 --> 01:32:48,528
Glenn?

2369
01:32:48,563 --> 01:32:51,117
Don'’t you do-
Don't do that.

2370
01:32:51,151 --> 01:32:54,396
Don't you even think
about doing that.

2371
01:32:54,430 --> 01:32:59,090
Glenn, don't.
Don't. Don't do it.

2372
01:32:59,125 --> 01:33:01,265
Don't do it Glenn.

2373
01:33:01,299 --> 01:33:03,647
Oh, stop. Glenn, no!

2374
01:33:04,786 --> 01:33:07,547
[screaming loudly]

2375
01:33:07,582 --> 01:33:10,723
He became Ted Turner
now. Colorize the movie.

2376
01:33:10,757 --> 01:33:12,483
Got shocked all
the way back to Oz.

2377
01:33:12,517 --> 01:33:16,107
Hmm-mmm. That's good voltage.

2378
01:33:18,109 --> 01:33:20,180
I don't think this method
is a final exit, guys.

2379
01:33:20,215 --> 01:33:23,977
Glenn, the Governor
called, oh nevermind.

2380
01:33:24,012 --> 01:33:26,739
He's just disappeared.
That happens.

2381
01:33:26,773 --> 01:33:28,672
- Let's us disappear.
- Okay.

2382
01:33:28,706 --> 01:33:30,259
- Shocking.
- Excuse me.

2383
01:33:33,953 --> 01:33:38,682
[doors closing hydraulically]

2384
01:33:44,411 --> 01:33:49,071
Oh boy, I gotta admit
guys, a movie that bad...

2385
01:33:49,106 --> 01:33:52,074
Yep, that was bad.
Bad City, Arizona.

2386
01:33:52,109 --> 01:33:53,110
Bad-o-rama.

2387
01:33:53,144 --> 01:33:56,044
I think it made
me kinda drowsy.

2388
01:33:56,078 --> 01:33:58,391
Bad to the b-b-bone.
Born to be bad.

2389
01:33:58,425 --> 01:33:59,703
Bad, bad, bad.

2390
01:33:59,737 --> 01:34:02,947
Born under a bad sign.
Been bad so damn long.

2391
01:34:02,982 --> 01:34:07,089
Try a piece. Taste anything?

2392
01:34:07,124 --> 01:34:09,885
I think I've tasted
something like that somewhere.

2393
01:34:09,920 --> 01:34:13,820
Tastes kinda like
Fritos, or tuna fish.

2394
01:34:13,855 --> 01:34:16,996
Yeah, I made it with my
little toe and permanent.

2395
01:34:17,030 --> 01:34:18,238
Joel, what?!

2396
01:34:18,273 --> 01:34:20,793
[spitting fiercely]

2397
01:34:20,827 --> 01:34:22,070
Give me a bite of that.

2398
01:34:22,104 --> 01:34:24,210
Yeah, Tom Servo,
you try some.

2399
01:34:25,660 --> 01:34:27,869
Hey, that one has
a tunnel of drugs.

2400
01:34:27,903 --> 01:34:29,249
Yeah, Phenobarbital.

2401
01:34:29,284 --> 01:34:31,010
Enough to knock you out
for about eight hours.

2402
01:34:31,044 --> 01:34:32,805
[yawning]

2403
01:34:32,839 --> 01:34:35,842
You guys, I'm just kidding.
We gotta read a letter.

2404
01:34:35,877 --> 01:34:37,568
It's always funny
until someone gets killed.

2405
01:34:37,602 --> 01:34:38,603
Yeah, in particularly.

2406
01:34:38,638 --> 01:34:40,605
That one hit hard. Anyway...

2407
01:34:40,640 --> 01:34:45,852
This one is a letter. Let's
put that on stillstore Cambot.

2408
01:34:45,887 --> 01:34:49,856
And it says: Greetings from
the Colorado State Prison.

2409
01:34:49,891 --> 01:34:51,927
A few of us guys
here watch your show.

2410
01:34:51,962 --> 01:34:54,689
Keeps us laughing, so please
keep up the good work.

2411
01:34:54,723 --> 01:34:56,794
Your show makes doing
time a little bit easier.

2412
01:34:56,829 --> 01:35:00,004
Your loyal fans,
Anthony, Dave, and Jack.

2413
01:35:00,039 --> 01:35:02,110
All right, that's cool.

2414
01:35:02,144 --> 01:35:03,905
Brave on.

2415
01:35:03,939 --> 01:35:05,872
Free your mind my brothers.

2416
01:35:05,907 --> 01:35:07,909
Anyway, over to you, Glenn.

2417
01:35:07,943 --> 01:35:11,533
OK. How does this work?

2418
01:35:11,567 --> 01:35:13,811
Put this up on
stillstore, Cambot,

2419
01:35:13,846 --> 01:35:17,366
or rocket number nine
I guess it would be.

2420
01:35:17,401 --> 01:35:23,269
And this one says: Dear Joel,
Tom Servo, Crow and Gypsy.

2421
01:35:23,303 --> 01:35:26,513
I like Crow because
he talks a lot.

2422
01:35:26,548 --> 01:35:27,722
I've heard it, he does.

2423
01:35:27,756 --> 01:35:30,725
No wonder he has
a big, long mouth.

2424
01:35:30,759 --> 01:35:35,143
And I like Gypsy because
she has a purple mouth.

2425
01:35:35,177 --> 01:35:37,386
But the guy I like
the most is Joel.

2426
01:35:37,421 --> 01:35:40,942
He sleeps during the
movies, who can blame him?

2427
01:35:40,976 --> 01:35:43,910
And I think Tom Servo is cute.

2428
01:35:43,945 --> 01:35:45,774
And there's a little
picture there.

2429
01:35:45,809 --> 01:35:48,604
This is from Deborah M.

2430
01:35:48,639 --> 01:35:49,813
That's a cute kid.

2431
01:35:49,847 --> 01:35:52,539
You know, I used to have
little fans like this.

2432
01:35:52,574 --> 01:35:54,852
Before my career
was cut short.

2433
01:35:54,887 --> 01:35:56,889
Hey Glenn,
thanks for the help.

2434
01:35:56,923 --> 01:35:59,754
Anytime, friend. I'll be
right here if you need me.

2435
01:35:59,788 --> 01:36:02,066
Sure thanks, and good luck.

2436
01:36:02,101 --> 01:36:04,448
Just give me a
holler. I won't be far.

2437
01:36:04,482 --> 01:36:06,830
You know, mi casa es su casa.

2438
01:36:06,864 --> 01:36:08,107
What a sweet guy.

2439
01:36:08,141 --> 01:36:09,936
Yeah. What do you think sirs?

2440
01:36:09,971 --> 01:36:14,285
Well Tom Thumb, I think
I'll be at the bakery.

2441
01:36:14,320 --> 01:36:16,943
And as for you, these
Thorazine waffles

2442
01:36:16,978 --> 01:36:20,498
should get your day off to
a heavily medicated start.

2443
01:36:20,533 --> 01:36:22,949
I had Jell-O today.

2444
01:36:22,984 --> 01:36:25,262
Just start running, Frank.

2445
01:36:27,367 --> 01:36:28,921
[explosion]

2446
01:36:28,955 --> 01:36:33,615
[slow synthesizer music]

